window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("infra-capacity-economics", {"topics":[{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","sources":["Official docs: NVIDIA GPU Operator","Official docs: NVIDIA Container Toolkit","Official docs: Kubernetes (device plugins and extended resources)"],"why":"CPU servers are interchangeable enough that most engineers never think about the box: any node runs\nany container, the OS hides the hardware, and a scheduler can place work anywhere. GPUs break every one\nof those assumptions. A GPU is a separate computer attached over PCIe with its own memory, its own\ninstruction set and its own driver, and the host kernel does not virtualise it the way it virtualises\nCPU time and RAM. An inference container therefore only works if the driver on the node, the CUDA\ntoolkit inside the image, the device plugin that advertised the card, and the scheduler's placement\nall line up.\n\nThe naive approach, \"install the driver, `pip install torch`, run it\", works on one developer machine\nand collapses at the second node: driver versions drift, images pull in CUDA builds the driver cannot\nrun, pods land on nodes without a card, two pods silently share one card and OOM each other, and nobody\ncan say whether the fleet is 30% or 90% busy because nothing exports GPU metrics. Add the price — one\nhigh-end accelerator costs more per hour than a rack of web servers — and the stack has to be\ndeliberate: every idle hour is wasted money and every misplaced pod is a capacity bug. GPU\ninfrastructure is the set of layers that turn a card into a reliable, schedulable, observable, billable\nresource.","how":"Walk the stack bottom-up. Each layer answers one question, and each has a lesson that goes deeper.\n\n### 1. The accelerator: what does the compute look like?\nA data-centre GPU is thousands of simple cores grouped into streaming multiprocessors (SMs), each with\ntensor cores for matrix maths, fed by on-package memory (HBM on A100/H100, GDDR6 on L4). Two numbers\ndominate everything downstream: memory capacity, which decides whether the model fits (see *GPU\nsizing*), and memory bandwidth, which bounds how fast decode can run (see *GPU utilization & memory\nbandwidth*). Form factor matters too: SXM modules sit on a baseboard with NVLink/NVSwitch between GPUs\nand run at higher power; PCIe cards slot into ordinary servers, draw less power, and talk to each other\nonly over PCIe unless bridged. Which card to buy is the subject of *A100 / H100 / L4 selection*.\n\n### 2. The node: what feeds the card?\nA GPU node is a host with one to eight accelerators plus the CPU, RAM, storage and network that keep\nthem busy. Each can be the bottleneck:\n- **CPU and host RAM.** Tokenisation, request handling, data loading and the serving engine's scheduler\n  run on the CPU. Host RAM must stage weights on the way to the device, so a 64 GB host struggles to\n  load a 140 GB checkpoint.\n- **Storage.** Weights are read from disk at start-up. Pulling 140 GB over a 100 MB/s network volume\n  takes about 23 minutes; from local NVMe at a few GB/s it takes under a minute. Cold-start time is\n  mostly a storage decision.\n- **Intra-node interconnect.** Tensor parallelism exchanges activations between GPUs every layer.\n  NVLink gives hundreds of GB/s per GPU (NVIDIA quotes 900 GB/s for H100 SXM); a PCIe Gen4 x16 link\n  gives roughly 32 GB/s per direction. Sharding across PCIe-only cards works but leaves bandwidth on the\n  table.\n- **Network.** Multi-node training needs RDMA (InfiniBand or RoCE) so gradient all-reduce does not\n  stall in the host networking stack; single-model serving rarely does.\n\n### 3. Driver and CUDA: will the code run at all?\nThe kernel-mode NVIDIA driver is installed once per node and is the only piece that touches hardware.\nEverything else — CUDA runtime, cuBLAS, cuDNN, NCCL, the PyTorch build — ships inside the container\nand must be a version the node's driver supports. Drivers are backward compatible with older CUDA\nruntimes, not forward compatible, so \"CUDA driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version\"\nmeans the image is newer than the node. Pin both, and upgrade drivers fleet-wide *before* bumping base\nimages.\n\n### 4. Container runtime: how does a container see the device?\nContainers do not include the driver. The NVIDIA Container Toolkit hooks the container runtime so\nthat, when a container requests GPUs, the device files (`/dev/nvidia*`) and the driver's user-space\nlibraries are mounted in at start. This is why a GPU image is portable across driver versions and why\n`nvidia-smi` works inside a container that never installed it.\n\n### 5. Scheduling: which workload gets which card?\nOn Kubernetes a device plugin advertises GPUs as an extended resource, `nvidia.com/gpu`, in integer\nunits. Extended resources cannot be overcommitted and requests must equal limits, *because* there is\nno kernel mechanism to time-share or throttle a GPU the way CFS shares CPU. The NVIDIA GPU Operator\npackages driver, toolkit, device plugin, feature discovery (node labels such as the card model) and the\nDCGM metrics exporter as one install. Taints keep ordinary pods off expensive nodes; labels let a pod\ntarget the right tier. Sharing one card between small workloads has three mechanisms with different\nisolation: MIG hardware-partitions an A100 or H100 into up to seven instances with separate memory;\ntime-slicing lets the plugin advertise N virtual GPUs that share memory with no isolation; MPS lets\nprocesses run kernels concurrently on one card. Only MIG protects one tenant from another's OOM.\n\n### 6. Procurement: who pays, and how?\nThe same card arrives as on-demand instances, reserved or committed-use discounts, spot/preemptible\ncapacity, bare-metal leases, GPU-specialist clouds, or serverless per-second billing. Quotas gate all\nof them; a fresh cloud account often cannot launch a single H100 without a request. The economics live\nin *cost modeling*, *TCO* and *serverless GPUs*; the operational rule is that procurement latency\n(days for quota, weeks for reservations) is part of capacity planning.\n\n### 7. Observability: is it actually busy?\nThe DCGM exporter publishes per-GPU utilisation, memory use, temperature, power, ECC and XID error\ncounts to Prometheus. Without it the fleet is a black box; with it you discover that \"100% utilised\"\noften means one kernel at a time on an otherwise idle chip, which the utilization lesson unpacks.","example":"A minimal Kubernetes Deployment that asks for one GPU on a node labelled with a card model, tolerates\nthe GPU taint, and fails fast if the driver and runtime disagree. The init container runs `nvidia-smi`,\nwhich only succeeds if the toolkit mounted the driver libraries, so a mis-provisioned node fails the\npod before the server tries to load 16 GB of weights.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: embedder\nspec:\n  replicas: 1\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: embedder } }\n  template:\n    metadata: { labels: { app: embedder } }\n    spec:\n      nodeSelector:\n        nvidia.com/gpu.product: NVIDIA-L4        # label written by GPU feature discovery\n      tolerations:\n      - key: nvidia.com/gpu\n        operator: Exists\n        effect: NoSchedule\n      initContainers:\n      - name: gpu-check\n        image: nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-base-ubuntu22.04\n        command: [\"nvidia-smi\"]                   # fails the pod if the device is not visible\n        resources: { limits: { nvidia.com/gpu: 1 } }\n      containers:\n      - name: server\n        image: ghcr.io/example/embedder:1.4       # ships its own CUDA runtime, never the driver\n        resources:\n          limits: { nvidia.com/gpu: 1 }           # integer; requests are forced equal to limits\n          requests: { cpu: \"4\", memory: 16Gi }    # CPU for tokenisation, RAM to stage weights\n        volumeMounts:\n        - { name: weights, mountPath: /models, readOnly: true }\n      volumes:\n      - name: weights\n        persistentVolumeClaim: { claimName: model-weights }   # NVMe-backed, not object storage\n```","tradeoffs":["SXM/NVLink nodes make multi-GPU sharding fast but come in fixed eight-card blocks; PCIe cards are cheaper and available singly, which wins until a model must be split across cards.","A GPU Operator or managed node pool makes the driver stack someone else's problem but ties you to their driver cadence; bare metal gives control and costs an ops team.","Sharing a card (MIG, time-slicing, MPS) raises utilisation for small models but caps per-tenant performance and complicates scheduling; it stops paying off once one model saturates the card.","Spot and preemptible capacity is far cheaper but can vanish mid-job; fine for checkpointed training and batch inference, wrong for latency-SLO serving unless on-demand fallback exists."],"pitfalls":["Image CUDA runtime newer than the node driver; symptom is CrashLoopBackOff on exactly the nodes that have not had the driver upgrade, with \"driver version is insufficient\" in the logs.","Loading weights from object storage or a slow network disk on every start; symptom is pods stuck in Running-but-not-Ready for tens of minutes while the readiness probe fails.","Time-slicing two models onto one card without checking combined memory; symptom is CUDA OOM errors in one deployment that correlate with traffic to another.","Judging fleet health by `nvidia-smi` GPU-Util; symptom is \"95% busy\" dashboards while tokens per second sit at a fraction of what the card can deliver.","Treating quota and node lead time as instant; symptom is the autoscaler requesting a node, the cloud answering \"insufficient capacity\", and the request queue growing for twenty minutes."],"when":"You do not choose whether to have GPU infrastructure; you choose how many layers to own. Managed node\npools with the GPU Operator, or a serverless GPU platform, are right when the team is small and the\nworkload is standard inference: the driver stack, device plugin and metrics come pre-wired. Own more\nlayers when you need a specific NVLink topology, custom kernels or drivers, bare-metal pricing, or\non-prem placement. Whatever the split, measure three things: pod ready-time (storage and image size),\nGPU hours used versus purchased (procurement), and DCGM SM activity (whether \"busy\" is real). In\nday-to-day work this shows up as a CrashLoopBackOff you must attribute to the right layer; in\ninterviews, as \"walk me through what happens between `kubectl apply` and the first token\", where\nnaming each layer and its failure mode is the expected answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the pod spec, pins CUDA and driver versions, mounts weights from fast storage, and reads DCGM metrics to tell a real bottleneck from an idle card.","architect":"Decides which layers to own versus buy, the node shapes (SXM vs PCIe, sharing policy), and the procurement mix of reserved, on-demand and spot capacity.","fde":"Audits a customer's cluster layer by layer — driver, toolkit, device plugin, taints, metrics — before promising that a model will run there on day one."},"check":{"q":"After a base-image bump, new inference pods crash on half the cluster with \"CUDA driver version\nis insufficient\" and run fine on the other half. What happened, and how would you structure the fleet\nso it cannot recur?","a":"The image now carries a CUDA runtime newer than the kernel driver on the older half of the\nnodes; drivers support older runtimes, not newer ones, so only un-upgraded nodes fail. Immediate fix:\nroll the image back or pin the pods to upgraded nodes with a label. Structural fix: manage drivers with\nthe GPU Operator or node-pool images so every node runs one known version, label nodes with the driver\nversion, roll driver upgrades fleet-wide before base-image upgrades, and add an init container that\nruns `nvidia-smi` so a mismatch fails fast and visibly instead of after a long weight load. Capacity\nalso matters: if half the fleet is unusable, the autoscaler should be told, not left to discover it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can `nvidia.com/gpu` not be overcommitted on Kubernetes the way CPU can?","options":["GPUs are too expensive for the scheduler to risk contention","There is no kernel mechanism to time-share or throttle a GPU, so the device plugin hands out whole devices","The NVIDIA driver forbids more than one process per card","Extended resources are only counted at node level, not pod level"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CPU is overcommittable because the kernel's scheduler can slice time between processes. A GPU has no such host-side control, so Kubernetes exposes it as an integer extended resource with requests equal to limits. Multiple processes per card are possible (MPS, time-slicing) but without memory isolation."},{"q":"Which sharing mechanism protects one tenant from another tenant's out-of-memory error on the same card?","options":["Time-slicing configured in the device plugin","CUDA MPS","MIG hardware partitions","Setting memory requests equal to limits on both pods"],"answer":2,"explanation":"MIG partitions an A100 or H100 into instances with their own memory and compute slices. Time-slicing and MPS share one memory space, so a leak in one process can starve the other. Pod memory limits govern host RAM, not device memory."},{"q":"A GPU container image contains the CUDA runtime but no driver. Where does the driver come from at run time?","options":["It is downloaded from the registry as a hidden image layer","PyTorch bundles a user-space driver in its wheel","The NVIDIA Container Toolkit mounts the host node's driver libraries and device files into the container","Kubernetes injects it through the device plugin's gRPC socket"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The kernel driver lives on the node and must match the kernel; the toolkit's runtime hook bind-mounts its user-space libraries and `/dev/nvidia*` into any container that requests GPUs. The device plugin only tells the scheduler which devices exist and which ones a container was allocated."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","sources":["Rajbhandari et al., 2020 — ZeRO: Memory Optimizations Toward Training Trillion Parameter Models","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM (engine arguments, gpu_memory_utilization)"],"why":"A GPU cannot page to disk the way a CPU process can. When the device runs out of memory, the engine\ndoes not slow down gracefully; it throws a CUDA out-of-memory error and the process dies, often\nminutes into start-up after loading most of a checkpoint. The opposite failure is quieter and more\nexpensive: a team provisions eight 80 GB cards for a model whose weights occupy 16 GB, then wonders\nwhy the bill is high and throughput is low. Both come from treating VRAM as a vibe instead of a sum.\n\nThe naive rule, \"the model needs about as much memory as its parameter count in GB\", is wrong in\nboth directions. In fp16 every parameter is two bytes, so a 70-billion-parameter model needs 140 GB\nfor weights alone, nearly twice the naive estimate. And weights are only the fixed part: every\nin-flight request grows a KV cache proportional to its context length, prefill needs transient\nactivation memory, and the CUDA context and engine allocations take space before the first request\narrives. For fine-tuning the picture flips again: gradients and optimizer state make a full fine-tune\nof that 70B model need hundreds of gigabytes more than serving it. VRAM math is the discipline of\nwriting each term down, with the model's actual shapes, before buying or promising anything.","how":"### The inference budget\n```\nVRAM        ≈ weights + KV cache + activations/workspace + runtime overhead\nweights      = n_params × bytes_per_param\nKV per token = 2 × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element\nKV cache     = KV per token × tokens in flight (summed over all concurrent sequences)\n```\nBytes per parameter: fp32 4, fp16/bf16 2, int8/fp8 1, int4 roughly 0.5 plus a few percent for\nscales and zero-points (call it 0.55). The factor 2 in the KV term is one key and one value vector;\n`n_kv_heads` rather than `n_heads` is why grouped-query attention matters: Llama-3 models use 8 KV\nheads against 32 or 64 query heads, which cuts the cache 4–8× relative to full multi-head attention.\n\nRuntime overhead is everything that is neither weights nor cache: the CUDA context, cuBLAS/cuDNN\nworkspaces, captured CUDA graphs and the engine's own buffers. Order of magnitude, illustrative, it\nis around a gigabyte per process, and it exists before any request. Activations during prefill scale\nwith (tokens in the batch × hidden size) per layer and are transient; engines bound them by capping\nhow many prompt tokens one forward pass may contain (chunked prefill), so the practical rule is to\nleave headroom rather than compute them exactly.\n\nEngines expose the budget directly. vLLM's `gpu_memory_utilization` (default 0.9) is the fraction of\nthe card the engine may use *because* other processes and driver allocations need the rest; it\nprofiles weights plus peak activations once at start-up and gives everything remaining to KV-cache\nblocks. That remaining space, divided by KV-per-token, is the total number of tokens the server can\nhold across all concurrent requests, which is the real capacity number.\n\n### Two models, worked\nLlama-3-8B: about 8.0B parameters, 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128. Weights in bf16: 16 GB.\nKV per token = 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 bytes = 128 KB.\n\nLlama-3-70B: about 70.6B parameters, 80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128. Weights in bf16: 141 GB.\nKV per token = 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 bytes = 320 KB.\n\nNow the fits, taking a 0.9 utilisation budget and ~1 GB overhead per card:\n- 8B bf16 on a 24 GB L4: 21.6 − 16 − 1 ≈ 4.6 GB for KV ≈ 35k tokens ≈ eight or nine 4k-token\n  conversations.\n- 8B bf16 on an 80 GB H100: 72 − 16 − 1 = 55 GB ≈ 420k tokens ≈ a hundred 4k conversations, at which\n  point memory bandwidth rather than memory capacity becomes the limit.\n- 70B bf16 on two 80 GB H100s: 144 − 141 − 2 ≈ 1 GB for KV ≈ 3k tokens *total*. It \"fits\" but can\n  barely serve one request; this is the case the formula catches and intuition misses.\n- 70B in fp8 on the same two cards: 144 − 71 − 2 = 71 GB ≈ 220k tokens, a comfortable server.\n- 70B in int4 (~39 GB) on one 80 GB card: 72 − 39 − 1 = 32 GB ≈ 100k tokens, with a quality cost that\n  must be evaluated, not assumed.\n\nA long-context request is the other surprise: one 128k-token prompt on the 8B model is 128k × 128 KB\n= 16 GB of cache, as much as the weights, and a single such request can evict everything else.\n\n### The training budget\nFull fine-tuning with mixed precision and Adam keeps, per parameter: 2 bytes of bf16 weights, 2 bytes\nof bf16 gradient, 4 bytes of fp32 master weight, and 4 + 4 bytes of Adam first and second moments, for\n16 bytes per parameter, the accounting the ZeRO paper uses. The 8B model therefore needs ~128 GB\nbefore activations, so it does not fit one 80 GB card and must be sharded (ZeRO/FSDP) across several.\nActivation memory adds batch × sequence × hidden per layer, and attention without a fused kernel adds\na sequence-squared term; gradient checkpointing trades roughly a third more compute for storing only\nlayer boundaries. LoRA changes the equation *because* the base weights are frozen: they cost 2 bytes\nper parameter with no gradient or optimizer state, and only the adapter (typically well under 1% of\nparameters) carries the 16-byte burden, so the 8B model fine-tunes in roughly 16 GB plus activations.\nQLoRA stores the frozen base in 4-bit, about 4.5 GB for 8B, which is what makes 24 GB cards viable.\nEight-bit optimizer implementations cut Adam's 8 bytes to 2 when the master copy is also dropped.\n\n### Multi-GPU arithmetic\nTensor parallelism divides both weights and KV cache by the TP degree, while each card keeps its own\noverhead; the head count must divide evenly (8 KV heads means TP up to 8 without replication).\nPipeline parallelism divides layers instead and is used when cards lack NVLink. Either way, \"fits on\nN cards\" means the per-card sum, not the total, is under budget.\n\n### Order of decisions\nPrecision first (it halves or quarters the dominant term), then card and count (capacity), then\n`max_model_len` (the worst-case single request), then concurrency (what remains ÷ KV per token ÷\ntypical context). Reverse the order and you will size the card to a concurrency figure that the\nprecision choice later invalidates.","example":"A twenty-line planner that reproduces the worked numbers above. It uses decimal gigabytes (1e9) to\nmatch how vendors quote card memory; engines report GiB, so expect a 7% difference in their logs.\n\n```python\nGB = 1e9\n\ndef kv_per_token(n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim, bytes_el=2):\n    return 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_el        # one K and one V per layer\n\ndef plan(name, params_b, n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim, vram_gb, n_gpus=1,\n         bytes_w=2, util=0.9, overhead_gb=1.0, ctx=4096):\n    weights = params_b * GB * bytes_w\n    budget = vram_gb * GB * util * n_gpus                       # engine's usable share\n    kv_room = budget - weights - overhead_gb * GB * n_gpus\n    tok = kv_per_token(n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim)\n    tokens = max(kv_room, 0) / tok\n    print(f\"{name:26s} weights={weights/GB:6.1f}GB KV/tok={tok/1024:.0f}KB \"\n          f\"KV room={kv_room/GB:5.1f}GB ~{tokens:,.0f} tok ≈ {tokens/ctx:.0f} seqs@{ctx}\")\n\nplan(\"8B bf16 / L4 24GB\",       8.0, 32, 8, 128, 24)             # ~35k tok, ~9 seqs\nplan(\"8B bf16 / H100 80GB\",     8.0, 32, 8, 128, 80)             # ~420k tok, ~100 seqs\nplan(\"70B bf16 / 2xH100\",      70.6, 80, 8, 128, 80, n_gpus=2)   # ~2k tok: unusable\nplan(\"70B fp8 / 2xH100\",       70.6, 80, 8, 128, 80, n_gpus=2, bytes_w=1)     # ~220k tok\nplan(\"70B int4 / 1xH100\",      70.6, 80, 8, 128, 80, bytes_w=0.55)            # ~100k tok\n```","tradeoffs":["Lower weight precision frees memory for cache and raises concurrency, but int4 costs measurable quality on some tasks and dequantisation kernels can slow decode; it pays when memory, not quality, is the binding constraint.","A higher `gpu_memory_utilization` gives more KV blocks, but leaves less slack for activation spikes and co-located processes; push it only on a card nothing else touches.","Tensor parallelism across more cards multiplies KV capacity and bandwidth, yet adds per-layer communication and per-card overhead; it stops helping once the model already fits with KV headroom.","LoRA and QLoRA make fine-tuning fit small cards at the cost of a smaller parameter update and, for QLoRA, slower steps from dequantisation; full fine-tuning is only worth its multi-card cost when the adapter measurably underperforms."],"pitfalls":["Counting weights and forgetting overhead and activations; symptom is an OOM at start-up or on the first long prompt even though \"the model fits\".","Sizing concurrency on the average context instead of `max_model_len`; symptom is sudden request preemption or queueing whenever a few long documents arrive together.","Assuming a fine-tune needs the same memory as serving; symptom is an OOM in the first optimizer step after the forward pass succeeded, because Adam state is allocated lazily.","Dividing total memory across cards instead of checking per-card budgets with TP; symptom is one rank OOMing while the others wait on NCCL.","Treating int4 as \"half of fp16\"; symptom is a card that fits the weights but throughput below the fp16 figure because dequantisation dominates at the batch sizes used."],"when":"Run the arithmetic every time a model, card, precision, context limit or concurrency target changes,\nand before any procurement conversation. The inputs are all public: parameter count and layer/head\nshapes from the model config, memory from the card datasheet. Reach for it when someone asks \"how many\nusers can this card serve\", \"why did we OOM at 2 a.m.\" or \"can we fine-tune this on what we have\".\nMeasure the engine's reported KV-block count against your estimate; a large gap means an overhead\nterm you missed. In interviews, deriving bytes-per-token from the config and turning it into a\nconcurrency figure, then adjusting for precision and TP, is the expected depth for both engineering\nand architecture roles.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes weights and KV per token from the model config before setting `max_model_len`, `gpu_memory_utilization` and batch limits, and validates against the engine's start-up log.","architect":"Owns the fit table — which models run on which card tier at which precision — and uses it to size clusters and to decide when quantisation or TP is mandatory.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"we have four A100s\" into which models and context lengths are realistic, and shows the arithmetic when a 70B request will not fit as asked."},"check":{"q":"You have one 80 GB card and a customer wants Llama-3-70B serving twenty concurrent 8k-token\nchats. Walk through whether it fits and what you would change.","a":"bf16 weights are 141 GB, so no. fp8 weights are 71 GB; with a 72 GB budget at 0.9 utilisation\nand ~1 GB overhead that leaves nothing for cache, so still no. int4 is about 39 GB, leaving ~32 GB\nfor KV at 320 KB per token, roughly 100k tokens; twenty chats at 8k need 160k, so about twelve fit.\nOptions: quantise the KV cache to 8-bit, doubling capacity to ~200k tokens, which meets the target if\nquality evaluation passes; cap concurrency at twelve and queue; or add a second card and run fp8 with\nTP=2 for ~220k tokens and better throughput. A strong answer also notes that twenty concurrent 8k\ncontexts will be bandwidth-bound on one card, so latency targets need checking too."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a full fine-tune need roughly eight times the memory of serving the same model in bf16?","options":["Training must keep a copy of every activation for the whole dataset","Gradients, fp32 master weights and two Adam moments add about 14 bytes per parameter on top of the 2-byte weights","Backpropagation requires the weights in fp64 for numerical stability","The KV cache must be stored for every training sequence simultaneously"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mixed-precision Adam keeps bf16 weights and gradients plus fp32 master weights and two fp32 moments, 16 bytes per parameter versus 2 for inference, before activations. Training does not use a KV cache and master weights are fp32, not fp64."},{"q":"Llama-3-70B in bf16 (141 GB) is loaded on two 80 GB cards with tensor parallelism. What is the practical problem?","options":["The head count is not divisible by two, so TP fails","NCCL cannot shard models larger than one card's memory","After weights and overhead only about a gigabyte remains for KV cache, so the server can hold only a few thousand tokens","bf16 is not supported across tensor-parallel ranks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The usable budget is about 144 GB at 0.9 utilisation; weights take 141 GB and overhead about 2 GB, leaving roughly 3k tokens of cache at 320 KB per token. The model technically loads but cannot serve real traffic, which is why fp8 or four cards are the usual choices."},{"q":"Which single change most increases the number of concurrent 4k-token requests an 8B bf16 model can hold on a 24 GB card?","options":["Raising the number of CPU threads for tokenisation","Switching from grouped-query to multi-head attention","Quantising the weights to int4, freeing roughly 11 GB for KV cache","Increasing `max_model_len` to 32k"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With ~4.5 GB of cache room, int4 weights (~4.4 GB instead of 16 GB) more than triple the space for KV blocks. Multi-head attention would multiply KV per token by four and reduce concurrency; a larger `max_model_len` changes the worst case per request, not the total capacity."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","sources":["Williams, Waterman & Patterson, 2009 — Roofline: An Insightful Visual Performance Model for Multicore Architectures","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Chowdhery et al., 2022 — PaLM: Scaling Language Modeling with Pathways (model FLOPs utilization)","Official docs: NVIDIA DCGM (profiling metrics)"],"why":"Teams buy GPUs for their FLOPs and are then surprised that an H100 serving a chat model produces\ntokens at a rate that would embarrass its spec sheet, while the dashboard shows 98% utilisation.\nBoth symptoms come from the same mistake: treating the GPU as a compute device when, for the workload\nin question, it is a memory device. Every decode step of a transformer must stream every weight byte\nfrom HBM into the compute units to produce one token per sequence. If only one sequence is in flight,\neach byte is used for a single multiply-add and the tensor cores sit idle most of the cycle, waiting on\nmemory.\n\nThe naive diagnosis, \"utilisation is high, so we need more GPUs\", spends money to replicate idle\nhardware. The naive fix, \"buy the card with the most TFLOPs\", buys the wrong axis. The roofline model\ngives the right question: how many operations does this workload perform per byte it moves (its\narithmetic intensity), and is that above or below the point where the card switches from\nbandwidth-limited to compute-limited? That one ratio explains why batching multiplies throughput\nalmost for free, why quantisation speeds up decode even when the math is not faster, why prefill and\ndecode behave like different workloads, and which utilisation metric to trust. Without it capacity\nplanning is guesswork dressed up as a dashboard.","how":"### The roofline\nA kernel's attainable throughput is the lower of two ceilings:\n```\nattainable FLOP/s = min( peak FLOP/s , arithmetic intensity × memory bandwidth )\narithmetic intensity = FLOPs performed / bytes moved from memory   (FLOP per byte)\nridge point          = peak FLOP/s / bandwidth                      (FLOP per byte)\n```\nBelow the ridge point the kernel is memory-bound and runs at `intensity × bandwidth`; above it, the\nkernel is compute-bound and runs near peak. Using NVIDIA's datasheet figures for dense bf16 tensor\nthroughput and memory bandwidth, the ridge sits around 312 TFLOP/s ÷ 2.0 TB/s ≈ 156 FLOP/byte for an\nA100 80 GB SXM, about 989 ÷ 3.35 ≈ 295 for an H100 SXM, and roughly 121 ÷ 0.3 ≈ 400 for an L4 (whose\nbandwidth is small relative to its compute). The number to remember is \"hundreds\": a workload must\nperform hundreds of operations per byte before the tensor cores are the bottleneck.\n\n### Decode intensity: why batch size is the lever\nIn one decode step, each weight is multiplied by one activation and accumulated: two FLOPs per\nparameter per sequence. In bf16 each parameter is two bytes and must be read once per step regardless\nof how many sequences share the step. So for a batch of *B* sequences the dense layers do about\n`2 × B` FLOPs per 2 bytes, an intensity of roughly *B* FLOP/byte. At *B* = 1 that is 1 FLOP/byte\nagainst a ridge of ~300: the H100 is using well under 1% of its arithmetic and is entirely limited by\nhow fast HBM can stream 16 GB of 8B-model weights. Doubling the batch doubles tokens per second at\nalmost no cost in per-token latency, until *B* approaches the ridge point. This is the whole case for\ncontinuous batching.\n\nThe lower bound on a decode step is therefore `bytes read ÷ bandwidth`. For an 8B bf16 model: 16 GB ÷\n3.35 TB/s ≈ 4.8 ms per step on an H100 (at most ~200 tokens/s for a single stream); on an L4 at\n300 GB/s it is ~53 ms (at most ~19 tokens/s). Real engines land below these bounds, but the ratio\nbetween cards holds, which is why decode speed tracks bandwidth rather than TFLOPs.\n\n### Attention does not batch the same way\nWeights are shared across sequences; KV caches are not. Each sequence's attention reads its own cache,\nso attention's bytes grow with *B* × context and its intensity stays low no matter the batch. With a\nhundred 4k-token sequences on the 8B model the step reads ~54 GB of cache (100 × 4096 × 128 KB) on top\nof 16 GB of weights, so the cache, not the weights, dominates the memory traffic. Grouped-query\nattention, KV quantisation and FlashAttention's tiling (which avoids writing the full attention matrix\nto HBM) all exist to reduce those bytes.\n\n### Prefill is the other regime\nPrefill processes all prompt tokens in one pass, so each weight byte serves every prompt token:\nintensity is roughly the number of tokens in the pass. A 2,000-token prompt is far above the ridge\nand compute-bound. That is why time-to-first-token scales with prompt length and TFLOPs, while\ntime-per-output-token scales with bandwidth, and why engines schedule prefill and decode differently.\n\n### What the utilisation metrics measure\n- `nvidia-smi` **GPU-Util** (DCGM `DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL`): fraction of the sample period in which *any*\n  kernel was executing. One tiny kernel per millisecond reads as 100%.\n- DCGM **SM activity** (`DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_ACTIVE`): fraction of time at least one warp was resident on\n  an SM; **SM occupancy** (`DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_OCCUPANCY`): how full the SMs were; **tensor pipe active**\n  (`DCGM_FI_PROF_PIPE_TENSOR_ACTIVE`): whether the matrix units did work; **DRAM active**\n  (`DCGM_FI_PROF_DRAM_ACTIVE`): memory bandwidth in use. A decode-bound server shows high DRAM\n  activity and low tensor activity; a prefill-heavy one shows the reverse.\n- **Model FLOPs utilisation (MFU)**: observed tokens/s × FLOPs per token ÷ peak FLOP/s. It is the\n  honest single number for training; for decode it is naturally low and should be read alongside\n  bandwidth utilisation (bytes moved per second ÷ peak bandwidth).\n\n### Other things that masquerade as GPU limits\nKernel-launch overhead when batches are tiny (CUDA graphs fix it), Python-side scheduling and\ntokenisation that leave the device starving, and host-to-device copies over PCIe. The tell is low DRAM\n*and* low tensor activity together: the GPU is waiting on the host, and no card upgrade will help.","example":"A small roofline estimator for decode. It takes datasheet bandwidth and peak compute, the bytes a step\nmust read (weights plus the KV cache of every sequence in the batch), and reports the bandwidth-bound\nstep time, the tokens/s ceiling and the batch at which the step becomes compute-bound. The outputs\nfor an 8B bf16 model reproduce the figures above; they are upper bounds, not measurements.\n\n```python\nCARDS = {  # datasheet-level figures, rounded: (HBM bandwidth B/s, dense bf16 FLOP/s)\n    \"L4\":       (300e9,  121e12),\n    \"A100-80\":  (2.0e12, 312e12),\n    \"H100-SXM\": (3.35e12, 989e12),\n}\n\ndef decode_step(card, params=8.0e9, bytes_per_param=2, batch=1, ctx=4096, kv_per_token=131072):\n    bw, peak = CARDS[card]\n    weight_bytes = params * bytes_per_param\n    kv_bytes = batch * ctx * kv_per_token              # every sequence reads its own cache\n    bytes_read = weight_bytes + kv_bytes\n    flops = 2 * params * batch                          # dense layers only; attention omitted\n    intensity = flops / bytes_read\n    t_mem, t_compute = bytes_read / bw, flops / peak\n    t = max(t_mem, t_compute)                           # roofline: the slower ceiling wins\n    ridge = peak / bw\n    print(f\"{card:9s} B={batch:4d} ctx={ctx:5d} read={bytes_read/1e9:6.1f} GB \"\n          f\"intensity={intensity:6.1f} ridge={ridge:4.0f}  step={t*1e3:6.1f} ms \"\n          f\"tok/s={batch/t:8.0f} bound={'memory' if t_mem >= t_compute else 'compute'}\")\n\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=1)                  # ~4.8 ms, ~200 tok/s, intensity ~1\ndecode_step(\"L4\", batch=1)                        # ~53 ms, ~19 tok/s\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=64)                 # weights amortised; KV now a large share\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=100, ctx=4096)      # ~54 GB of KV per step dominates\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=8, bytes_per_param=0.55)  # int4: fewer bytes, faster step\n```","tradeoffs":["Larger batches raise tokens/s nearly linearly while memory-bound, but each sequence's latency grows once the step becomes compute-bound or KV traffic dominates; the flip is near the ridge point or when cache bytes exceed weight bytes.","Weight quantisation cuts bytes per step and speeds decode, yet dequantisation kernels can erase the gain at large batches where compute, not bandwidth, is the limit.","Speculative decoding converts spare compute into more tokens per weight read, which pays when decode is bandwidth-bound and acceptance rates are high, and costs throughput otherwise.","A faster-memory card (H100 over A100) buys decode speed roughly in proportion to bandwidth, not price; for prefill-heavy or training workloads the FLOPs gap matters more."],"pitfalls":["Reading `nvidia-smi` GPU-Util as \"busy\"; symptom is a fleet at 95% utilisation that delivers a small fraction of the tokens/s the bandwidth bound predicts.","Scaling out on utilisation instead of on queue depth or tokens/s; symptom is more cards, same per-card throughput, and a bill that doubled.","Benchmarking at batch 1 and extrapolating; symptom is a capacity plan off by an order of magnitude because batching was never measured.","Ignoring attention's share of memory traffic; symptom is throughput collapsing as contexts get long, even though weights never changed.","Blaming the GPU for host-side stalls; symptom is low DRAM *and* low tensor activity together, with a single Python process pegged at 100% CPU."],"when":"Reach for the roofline whenever throughput is below expectation, when choosing between cards, or when\nsomeone proposes \"more GPUs\" as the fix. Compute the bytes a decode step must read, divide by\nbandwidth, and compare with observed time-per-output-token: if they are close you are bandwidth-bound\nand need batching, quantisation or faster memory; if observed time is far worse, look at the host,\nkernel launches or scheduling. Monitor DCGM SM activity, tensor activity and DRAM activity rather than\nGPU-Util, and track tokens/s per card as the business metric. In interviews, \"why is decode\nmemory-bound and what does batching do to it\" is a standard question; the strong answer derives the\nintensity of ~B FLOP/byte and names the ridge point.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tunes batch limits, enables CUDA graphs and quantisation, and uses DCGM profiling metrics to prove whether a slow server is bandwidth-, compute- or host-bound.","architect":"Chooses cards by bandwidth for decode-heavy services and by FLOPs for training or prefill-heavy ones, and sets capacity targets in tokens/s per card rather than utilisation.","fde":"Shows a customer why their \"fully utilised\" cluster is idle in practice, and sizes the batching change that recovers throughput before recommending new hardware."},"check":{"q":"A customer's H100 serves an 8B bf16 model at 40 tokens/s per request with four concurrent\nrequests, and `nvidia-smi` shows 97% utilisation. They want to buy three more H100s. What do you tell\nthem?","a":"The bandwidth bound for one decode step is about 16 GB ÷ 3.35 TB/s ≈ 5 ms, so a single stream\ncould approach 200 tokens/s and a batch of four should deliver far more than 160 aggregate. At 40\ntokens/s per stream the step takes ~25 ms, five times the bound, and GPU-Util at 97% only means some\nkernel was always running. Check DCGM: low DRAM activity and low tensor activity point to host-side\nstalls, kernel-launch overhead or a misconfigured engine, which more cards will not fix. Raise\nconcurrency and batch size, enable CUDA graphs, and confirm the engine is using fused attention.\nBuy hardware only after per-card tokens/s approaches the bandwidth bound at the target batch."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does doubling the batch size roughly double decode throughput on a bandwidth-bound GPU?","options":["The GPU clocks up under heavier load","Weights are read from memory once per step and shared by every sequence in the batch, so the dominant cost is amortised","Larger batches compress the KV cache","Tensor cores only activate above a minimum batch size"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A decode step must stream all weights from HBM regardless of batch size; with more sequences each byte does more useful work, raising arithmetic intensity. KV traffic still grows per sequence, so the gain is not unlimited."},{"q":"Which DCGM signal most directly reveals that a serving GPU is memory-bandwidth-bound?","options":["High GPU-Util with low power draw","High SM occupancy","High DRAM activity together with low tensor-pipe activity","Low PCIe transmit throughput"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bandwidth-bound decode keeps the memory system busy while the matrix units wait, so DRAM activity is high and tensor activity low. GPU-Util cannot distinguish that state from a fully compute-bound one."},{"q":"Prefill of a 3,000-token prompt and decoding of its reply run on the same GPU. Which statement is correct?","options":["Both phases are memory-bound because they read the same weights","Prefill is compute-bound because every weight serves thousands of tokens per pass, while decode is memory-bound at one token per sequence per step","Decode is compute-bound because it runs more steps","Prefill is memory-bound because it must write the whole KV cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Arithmetic intensity in prefill is roughly the number of tokens in the pass, far above the ridge point; in decode it is roughly the batch size, usually far below it. Writing the KV cache is small relative to the matrix multiplications over thousands of tokens."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection","sources":["Official docs: NVIDIA A100, H100 and L4 datasheets","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference"],"why":"Three cards from the same vendor can differ by an order of magnitude in price and in decode speed,\nand the cheapest option per hour is frequently the most expensive per token, or simply cannot run the\nmodel at all. Teams that pick by hourly price end up with L4s that cannot hold a 70B model or that\ndeliver 15 tokens per second to a chat user; teams that pick by prestige put an 8B internal tool on\nan H100 that idles 95% of the day. Neither error shows up until the bill or the latency complaints\narrive.\n\nThe naive heuristic, \"buy the newest card\", fails because inference is usually bound by memory\ncapacity and bandwidth, not by the compute that generation-over-generation marketing emphasises, and\nbecause the cost of a token depends on how full the card is, which depends on your traffic, not on\nthe card. The other naive heuristic, \"buy the cheapest card that fits\", fails because fitting is not\nserving: a card that holds the weights with no room for KV cache serves one user, and a card with\n300 GB/s of bandwidth cannot reach interactive decode speeds on anything beyond a few billion\nparameters. Selection is therefore a short, repeatable procedure over four numbers — memory,\nbandwidth, price and expected load — followed by a feature checklist. This lesson gives the\nprocedure and the datasheet facts it runs on.","how":"### The cards, by the numbers that matter\nRounded figures from NVIDIA's datasheets; variants differ (A100 40 GB has ~1.55 TB/s, H100 PCIe\n~2 TB/s and lower TFLOPs, H100 NVL 94 GB, H200 141 GB at ~4.8 TB/s), so confirm the exact SKU.\n\n| | L4 | A100 80 GB SXM | H100 80 GB SXM |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Architecture | Ada Lovelace | Ampere | Hopper |\n| Memory | 24 GB GDDR6 | 80 GB HBM2e | 80 GB HBM3 |\n| Bandwidth | ~300 GB/s | ~2.0 TB/s | ~3.35 TB/s |\n| Dense bf16 tensor | ~120 TFLOP/s | ~312 TFLOP/s | ~990 TFLOP/s |\n| FP8 | yes | no | yes (Transformer Engine) |\n| MIG partitions | no | up to 7 | up to 7 |\n| Interconnect | PCIe only, no NVLink | NVLink 600 GB/s (SXM) | NVLink 900 GB/s (SXM) |\n| Power / form | 72 W, single-slot PCIe | 400 W SXM (PCIe variant 300 W) | up to 700 W SXM (PCIe 350 W) |\n\nThe bandwidth column is the decode column: the H100 streams weights ~11× faster than the L4 and\n~1.7× faster than the A100, and single-stream tokens/s follow those ratios almost directly. The\nmemory column is the fit column. The FP8 row matters *because* fp8 weights halve the bytes per decode\nstep and double the KV room with far less quality loss than int4; the A100 cannot use it and must\nfall back to int8 weight-only or int4 schemes. MIG matters for packing many small models onto one\ncard with isolation; the L4 cannot do it, but at its price you simply buy more L4s.\n\n### The procedure\n1. **Fit.** Weights at your precision plus KV headroom, from the sizing lesson. Roughly: an L4 holds\n   an 8B model in bf16 with a few concurrent 4k contexts, or a ~13B model in int4; an 80 GB card holds\n   8B with hundreds of contexts, 30B-class models in bf16, or 70B in int4; 70B in fp8 or bf16 needs\n   two or four 80 GB cards with NVLink. Anything that does not fit ends the discussion.\n2. **Per-stream latency.** Decode step time is at least `bytes per step ÷ bandwidth`. For 8B bf16:\n   ~53 ms on L4 (≤19 tokens/s), ~8 ms on A100 (≤125), ~5 ms on H100 (≤200). If the SLO needs\n   30 tokens/s per user, the L4 is out regardless of price.\n3. **Cost per token at your load.** Estimate the throughput ceiling at the batch your memory allows,\n   multiply by expected utilisation, divide price by it. The expensive card wins when traffic keeps\n   it busy; the cheap card wins when traffic is sparse. The example below runs this.\n4. **Features.** FP8 (H100, L4) for cheap precision reduction; MIG (A100, H100) for isolated sharing;\n   NVLink (SXM) for tensor parallelism; L4's low power and single-slot size for edge boxes and ordinary\n   servers; L4's video encode/decode engines for vision pipelines.\n5. **Availability and procurement.** Quotas, regions, reservation terms and spot pricing differ by\n   card; A100 supply is typically easier than H100, and L4s are widely available in small counts.\n   Cloud families to look for: AWS p4d/p4de (A100 40/80 GB), p5 (H100), g6 (L4); GCP a2 (A100),\n   a3 (H100), g2 (L4); Azure ND A100 v4 and ND H100 v5. Check the current catalogue before planning.\n\n### Training versus serving\nTraining and full fine-tuning are compute- and interconnect-bound: H100's bf16 and fp8 throughput and\nNVLink make it the default, A100 remains cost-effective for smaller runs, and the L4 is only sensible\nfor LoRA on small models because 24 GB and PCIe-only links cap everything else. For serving, step 2\nand 3 dominate, which is why the \"A100 is obsolete\" claim is wrong for inference: its bandwidth is\n60% of an H100's at a typically lower price, and for models under 30B it is often the best value.\n\n### Neighbours you will see\nA10G (24 GB) and L40S (48 GB) sit around the L4 in price and above it in bandwidth; H200 is an H100\nwith more and faster memory for long contexts; newer generations exist and follow the same procedure\nwith their own datasheet numbers.","example":"An 8B bf16 chat model, two traffic levels, illustrative on-demand prices of roughly $0.80 per hour\nfor an L4 and $4.00 per hour for an H100 (ballpark only; prices vary by provider and over time).\n\n*Ceilings.* On the L4, KV room allows about eight 4k-token contexts, so batch 8: a step reads 16 GB\nof weights plus ~4.3 GB of cache, ≈ 68 ms at 300 GB/s, so ≈ 120 tokens/s aggregate. On the H100,\nKV room allows about a hundred contexts: a step reads 16 GB plus ~54 GB of cache, ≈ 21 ms at\n3.35 TB/s, so ≈ 4,800 tokens/s aggregate. Both are upper bounds.\n\n*Cost per million tokens at full load.* L4: 120 × 3,600 ≈ 430k tokens per hour, so $0.80 ÷ 0.43M ≈\n$1.90 per million. H100: 4,800 × 3,600 ≈ 17M tokens per hour, $4.00 ÷ 17M ≈ $0.23 per million. At full\nload the H100 is roughly eight times cheaper per token despite costing five times more per hour.\n\n*Scenario A, internal tool:* 0.3 requests/s averaging 300 output tokens is ~90 tokens/s, which keeps\nthe L4 at about 75% of its ceiling and the H100 at under 2%. Hourly cost is what you pay: $0.80 versus\n$4.00; the L4 wins five to one, and per-user decode (≤19 tokens/s) is acceptable for an internal\ntool.\n\n*Scenario B, public product:* 10 requests/s averaging 300 tokens is 3,000 tokens/s. That needs 25 L4s\n($20/h) or one H100 at ~60% of ceiling ($4/h), and the H100 users also get ~10× faster decode.\n\nThe crossover is wherever the H100's higher hourly price is covered by the tokens it actually serves:\nhere, somewhere around 500 tokens/s of sustained demand. Below it buy L4s; above it buy H100s; and\nif the demand is bursty, read the serverless lesson before buying either.","tradeoffs":["H100 delivers the lowest cost per token and fastest decode, but only when load keeps it busy; at low or bursty utilisation its hourly price makes the L4 or an API provider cheaper.","A100 is often the best value for sub-30B serving and small training runs, but its lack of FP8 forces int8/int4 for memory relief, with the quality evaluation that implies.","L4 is cheap, low-power and easy to get, yet its 300 GB/s bandwidth caps per-stream decode and its 24 GB caps model size, so it flips from \"obvious choice\" to \"cannot do it\" at roughly 8B–13B.","SXM nodes with NVLink make tensor parallelism efficient for 70B-class models, at the cost of buying eight-card blocks; PCIe cards are flexible but make multi-card sharding slow."],"pitfalls":["Choosing by hourly price alone; symptom is a fleet of L4s that cannot meet a per-user tokens/s SLO or cannot load the model the product team actually wants.","Choosing by TFLOPs for an inference workload; symptom is paying H100 prices for decode throughput that an A100 would have delivered at 60%.","Forgetting the A100 has no FP8; symptom is a quantisation plan validated on H100 that must be redone with int8/int4 kernels and re-evaluated for quality.","Sizing to the 80 GB figure without KV headroom; symptom is a 70B bf16 model that loads on two cards and serves one request at a time.","Ignoring quota and region availability; symptom is an architecture built around H100s that cannot be launched where the data must stay."],"when":"Run the five-step procedure whenever a new model, SLO or traffic forecast arrives, and re-run it when\nprices or quotas change. Reach for the L4 for embeddings, rerankers, vision models, small LLMs and\nanything with sparse traffic; for the A100 when a 7B–30B model needs real concurrency at the best\nprice, or when H100 quota is unavailable; for the H100 for 70B-class serving, fp8, training, or any\nservice whose sustained load would keep it busy. Measure tokens/s per card against the bandwidth\nceiling and the actual utilisation against the plan. In interviews, expect \"which card for X and\nwhy\"; the strong answer names the binding constraint (fit, bandwidth or cost per token) before naming\nthe card.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Benchmarks the candidate card at the real batch size and context, confirms fp8 or int4 kernels exist for the engine, and reports tokens/s per dollar rather than utilisation.","architect":"Owns the card-tier matrix (which workloads go on L4, A100, H100), the procurement mix, and the policy for when a workload must move tiers.","fde":"Maps a customer's available hardware and quotas to what each model can realistically do there, and defends the recommendation with fit and bandwidth arithmetic."},"check":{"q":"A customer runs a 13B model in bf16 for 200 daily-active users and asks whether to buy L4s,\nA100s or H100s. Reason it through.","a":"Fit first: 13B in bf16 is ~26 GB of weights, so the L4 is out unless the model is quantised to\nint4 (~7 GB), which needs a quality evaluation. Both 80 GB cards fit it with tens of gigabytes of KV\nheadroom. Latency next: decode on the A100 is bounded near 26 GB ÷ 2 TB/s ≈ 13 ms per step, around\n75 tokens/s per stream, comfortably interactive; the H100 would be ~1.7× faster. Load: 200 daily\nusers is likely a few requests per second at peak, well inside one A100's ceiling, so a second H100's\nthroughput would sit idle. Recommendation: one A100 80 GB (two for redundancy), or an int4 L4 pair if\nthe quality evaluation passes and budget dominates; revisit if traffic grows past the A100's ceiling\nor fp8 becomes necessary."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which card property most directly determines single-stream decode tokens per second for a given model and precision?","options":["Peak bf16 TFLOP/s","Memory bandwidth","Number of streaming multiprocessors","PCIe generation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each decode step must stream all weights (and the sequence's KV cache) from memory, so the step time floor is bytes ÷ bandwidth. TFLOPs matter for prefill and training, and PCIe only affects host transfers and multi-card traffic."},{"q":"A team validated fp8 serving of a 70B model on H100s and now must deploy on A100s. What changes?","options":["Nothing; fp8 checkpoints run on any card with 80 GB","The A100 runs fp8 but without tensor-core acceleration","The A100 has no fp8 support, so they must switch to int8 or int4 weight quantisation and re-evaluate quality","They must use the A100 40 GB variant, which supports fp8"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FP8 tensor cores arrived with Hopper and Ada (H100, L4); Ampere lacks them. The memory relief must come from integer quantisation schemes with different kernels and different quality behaviour, which invalidates the earlier evaluation."},{"q":"An internal tool serves an 8B model at 0.3 requests per second. Why is an L4 usually the better choice than an H100 here?","options":["The L4 has higher memory bandwidth for small models","The H100 cannot run models smaller than 30B efficiently","At that load the H100 would sit almost idle, so its higher hourly price buys throughput nobody uses, while the L4's decode speed is adequate","The L4 supports MIG and the H100 does not"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cost per token depends on utilisation. The H100 is far cheaper per token at full load, but at a few percent utilisation the hourly price dominates and the L4's lower bandwidth still meets an internal tool's latency needs. The L4 does not support MIG."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/serverless-gpus","sources":["Official docs: Modal","Official docs: RunPod Serverless","Official docs: Google Cloud Run (GPU support)"],"why":"A dedicated GPU costs the same per hour whether it serves ten thousand requests or none. Most\ninternal tools, prototypes, batch jobs and early-stage products have traffic that is bursty, diurnal\nor simply small: a document-processing pipeline that runs for two hours each night, an internal\nassistant used during working hours in one time zone, a feature with a hundred requests a day. Paying\nfor 730 hours a month to use 20 of them is the single largest waste line in many AI budgets, and it\nis invisible because the card shows up as \"in use\".\n\nThe naive fix, \"autoscale the Kubernetes deployment to zero\", runs into the fact that a GPU node\ntakes minutes to provision, pull a multi-gigabyte image and load weights; by the time the pod is\nReady the user has gone, and the cluster autoscaler may not even have the quota to add a node. The\nother naive fix, \"keep one replica warm\", is just a dedicated GPU with extra steps. Serverless GPU\nplatforms exist to make the scale-from-zero path fast enough to be usable — pre-provisioned fleets,\ncached images, weight caches on fast storage, process snapshots — and to bill at the granularity\n(seconds) that makes bursty use cheap. They do not remove the cold start; they shrink it and hide the\nnode layer. Knowing what remains, and what it costs, is the engineering content of this topic.","how":"### The request path\nA request arrives at the platform's router. If a warm container for your function exists and has\nspare concurrency, it runs there immediately. Otherwise the platform takes a GPU from its pooled\nfleet (it keeps nodes and often images pre-warmed *because* node provisioning is the slowest step and\namortises across tenants), starts your container, runs your initialisation, and only then serves the\nrequest. After the container has been idle for a configurable scale-down window, it is stopped and\nbilling ends. Scaling out is the same path repeated: when in-flight requests exceed per-container\nconcurrency, another container is started.\n\n### Anatomy of a cold start\nEach stage has its own mitigation, and the total is the sum:\n1. **Scheduling and container start.** Seconds on a pooled fleet; minutes if a node must be created.\n2. **Image pull.** A multi-gigabyte CUDA image can take tens of seconds; lazy-loading image formats\n   and keeping weights *out* of the image reduce this.\n3. **Process start and imports.** Importing PyTorch, CUDA initialisation and an inference engine's\n   setup take several seconds. Memory snapshots (checkpointing the process after imports, offered by\n   some platforms) skip this on later starts; GPU-state snapshots are newer and provider-specific.\n4. **Weight load.** Storage to host RAM to VRAM. 16 GB of bf16 weights from a local NVMe cache takes\n   seconds; from a remote bucket it can take minutes. Platforms offer volumes or model caches on fast\n   storage precisely to move this term from minutes to seconds; a network bucket on every start is\n   the classic mistake.\n5. **Engine warm-up.** CUDA graph capture, kernel compilation, `torch.compile`: tens of seconds for\n   some engines. Either accept it, disable it for serverless, or snapshot after it completes.\nOrder of magnitude, illustrative: a tuned small-model function cold-starts in 10–30 seconds; an\nuntuned 70B deployment can take ten minutes. Measure yours; it is the key input to every decision\nbelow.\n\n### The knobs\n- **Scale-down window (idle timeout).** Longer keeps containers warm through traffic gaps at the cost\n  of paying for idle seconds; shorter saves money and causes more cold starts.\n- **Minimum warm containers.** Removes cold starts for the base load, billed continuously at the\n  serverless rate, which is usually above on-demand; this is where \"serverless\" quietly becomes\n  \"expensive dedicated\".\n- **Concurrency per container.** Letting one container handle several requests at once lets the\n  engine batch them, raising throughput per GPU-second; set it from the batching lesson, not to 1.\n- **GPU type.** Most platforms let you pick L4/A10-class through H100; the selection lesson's\n  arithmetic applies unchanged, except that per-second billing makes low utilisation free.\n\n### Billing shape\nYou pay per second of container lifetime (not per request), often for CPU and memory as well, at a\nGPU rate above on-demand hourly pricing: the premium funds the idle pool and the fast storage.\nCold-start seconds are billed. Egress, storage volumes and request overhead may be separate lines.\n\n### What it is not\nServerless GPUs are *your container on their pooled GPUs*. Per-token model APIs are a different\nlayer: no container, no GPU choice, billed per token; they are the right answer for standard open\nmodels at modest scale. And not every \"serverless\" product has GPUs: AWS Lambda does not, and\nSageMaker's serverless inference option has been CPU-only; Google Cloud Run and Azure Container Apps\nhave added GPU options, with constraints that change, so check current support before designing\naround any of them.","example":"An 8B model on an L4-class GPU. Illustrative prices, ballpark only: dedicated on-demand $0.80 per\nhour ($584 for a 730-hour month); serverless $1.60 per hour-equivalent, billed per second, a 2×\npremium. Measured cold start after tuning: 45 seconds. Scale-down window: 5 minutes.\n\n*Workload A, internal document tool:* 1,000 requests per day at ~3 GPU-seconds each is 3,000 busy\nseconds a day, ~25 hours a month, $40. Requests cluster in working hours with gaps, producing about\n40 cold starts a day: 40 × 45 s = 30 minutes a day, ~15 hours a month, $24. Idle time inside the\n5-minute windows adds perhaps as much again. Serverless total: roughly $90 against $584 dedicated,\na six-fold saving, and the 45-second cold start is tolerable for a tool that already takes seconds.\n\n*Workload B, steady product traffic at 40% busy:* 292 busy hours × $1.60 = $467, cold starts\nnegligible because containers stay warm. Serverless still wins by 20%. The break-even is where the\nrates cross: 0.80 ÷ 1.60 = 50% utilisation, minus whatever cold starts and idle windows add. At 60%\nbusy, serverless costs $700 against $584 and dedicated wins.\n\n*Workload B with a latency SLO:* p95 response under 2 seconds. Even a 3% cold-start rate puts\n45-second responses inside the p95, which fails the SLO. Keeping one container always warm costs\n730 × $1.60 = $1,168, double the dedicated card. The right design is a dedicated card (or\nKubernetes replica) for the base load and serverless only for bursts above it, where the cold start\naffects a small tail of requests during ramps. The arithmetic, not the branding, picks the layout.","tradeoffs":["Per-second billing makes low and bursty utilisation cheap, but the rate premium makes steady high utilisation dearer than on-demand; the flip is near the ratio of the two prices.","Scale-to-zero removes idle cost but introduces cold starts; it is right when the SLO tolerates them or traffic gaps are rare, wrong for tight tail-latency targets.","Warm minimums and long idle windows buy latency with money, and past a point you are paying dedicated prices for a platform you do not control.","Managed pooling hides nodes, drivers and images, at the cost of vendor-specific SDKs, less control over GPU topology and limits on multi-GPU or multi-node models."],"pitfalls":["Loading weights from a remote bucket on every start; symptom is cold starts of minutes that no scale-down tuning fixes, visible as a bimodal latency histogram.","Baking weights into the container image; symptom is image pulls that take longer than the model load and rebuilds for every checkpoint change.","Setting per-container concurrency to one; symptom is many containers each running a batch of one, so GPU-seconds per request are several times higher than on a batched dedicated server.","Forgetting that cold-start seconds are billed; symptom is a bill where start-up time rivals serving time, which happens whenever the scale-down window is short relative to request gaps.","Promising a tight p95 on a scale-to-zero deployment; symptom is an SLO breach on the first request after every lull."],"when":"Reach for serverless GPUs when utilisation would be low or bursty (internal tools, nightly batch,\nprototypes, long-tail models that are rarely hit), when latency can absorb a cold start or the\nplatform's warm pool makes it rare, and when you want no node operations. Prefer dedicated capacity\nor a Kubernetes deployment when sustained utilisation exceeds the price ratio, when tail latency is\ncontractual, or when the model spans multiple GPUs. Measure your cold start by stage, your busy\nseconds per day and your p95 with cold starts included; those three numbers decide. In day-to-day\nwork this is the \"why did the first request take a minute\" ticket; in interviews, the expected\nanswer walks the cold-start anatomy and the break-even calculation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Structures the function so imports and weight loading happen once at container start, keeps weights on a cached volume, tunes concurrency and idle windows, and measures cold-start stages.","architect":"Decides which workloads live on serverless, which on dedicated capacity, and where the hybrid boundary sits, using measured utilisation and the SLO rather than vendor claims.","fde":"Prototypes a customer pilot on serverless to avoid procurement delays, then presents the utilisation data that justifies moving to dedicated hardware when traffic becomes steady."},"check":{"q":"A customer's serverless LLM endpoint shows p50 latency of 1.2 s and p95 of 48 s. They ask\nwhether to raise the minimum warm containers to five. What do you investigate and recommend?","a":"A 48-second p95 against a 1.2-second p50 is a cold-start signature: at least 5% of requests\nstart a container. First measure the cold start by stage (image pull, imports, weight load, warm-up)\nand fix the cheap ones: weights on a cached volume instead of a bucket, snapshotting after imports,\ndisabling or pre-capturing warm-up. Then look at traffic: if gaps are frequent, a longer scale-down\nwindow may cut cold starts more cheaply than warm containers. If base load is steady, one warm\ncontainer removes most cold starts; five warm containers is dedicated pricing for five cards and\nprobably exceeds an on-demand deployment. Recommend tuning first, then one warm container, and move\nthe base load to dedicated capacity if the utilisation arithmetic says so."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do serverless GPU platforms charge a per-second rate above on-demand hourly pricing?","options":["Per-second billing is more expensive to meter","The premium pays for the idle pooled fleet, cached images and fast storage that make scale-from-zero fast enough to use","GPUs run at higher clocks in serverless mode","Serverless containers get larger VRAM allocations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The platform must keep provisioned GPUs and warm infrastructure waiting for tenants' bursts; that idle capacity is shared and amortised through the rate premium. Customers accept it because they pay only for busy seconds."},{"q":"Which change most reduces cold-start time for a function whose 16 GB weights currently download from object storage on every start?","options":["Increasing per-container concurrency","Choosing an H100 instead of an L4","Keeping the weights on a platform volume or local cache so they load from fast storage","Shortening the scale-down window"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Weight loading from remote storage is usually the dominant cold-start stage; moving it to cached NVMe-class storage cuts minutes to seconds. A faster GPU does not speed a network download, and a shorter idle window causes more cold starts, not faster ones."},{"q":"At what utilisation does serverless stop being cheaper than a dedicated card, ignoring cold starts?","options":["Always above 10%, because of the premium","Never; per-second billing is always cheaper","Roughly where busy fraction equals the dedicated hourly rate divided by the serverless hourly-equivalent rate","At 100%, when both bill the same hours"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With a 2× premium the crossover is 50% busy; cold starts and idle windows are billed too, so the real crossover is lower. Above it the dedicated card's constant price covers more tokens."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Zhong et al., 2024 — DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized LLM Serving","Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: Kubernetes"],"why":"One model server on one GPU has a hard ceiling: weights plus KV cache must fit in VRAM, decode is\nbound by memory bandwidth, and the number of concurrent sequences that fit sets both throughput and\ntail latency. Once traffic exceeds what one replica serves within its latency budget, the only moves\nare a bigger GPU (there is a largest one) or more replicas. More replicas means a cluster, and a\ncluster immediately raises questions that a stateless web fleet never had to answer.\n\nThe naive answer is \"put the model servers behind the same load balancer we use for the API tier and\nautoscale on CPU\". It fails for three reasons. First, requests are not interchangeable: one request\nmay be a 200-token chat turn and the next a 60,000-token document summary; round-robin sends the\nlong one to a replica that is already saturated and the tail latency of everyone on that replica\ncollapses. Second, the useful capacity signal is not CPU or even GPU utilization but how full the KV\ncache is and how deep the engine's waiting queue is, since a GPU at 40% SM utilization can still be\nunable to admit another sequence. Third, the cache makes replicas stateful: a request whose prefix\nis cached on replica A costs a full prefill on replica B, so routing affects cost, not just balance.\nAlso, a model that needs more memory than one GPU has (a 70B model in fp16 is ~140 GB of weights)\nmust be split across several GPUs that act as one replica, which is a topology decision, not a\nscaling knob. An inference cluster is the structure that answers all of these on purpose.","how":"### The layers\nA cluster is organised as nodes → replicas → engines, with a router in front and a scheduler\nbehind. Each layer answers a different question.\n\n1. **Replica.** One running instance of a serving engine with one copy of the model. If the model\n   does not fit on one GPU, the replica spans several GPUs on the same node using tensor parallelism\n   (each layer's matrices are sliced across GPUs and the shards exchange activations every layer).\n   TP stays within a node *because* it needs an all-reduce per layer per token, which is only\n   affordable over NVLink-class bandwidth; crossing the network for it would make every decode\n   step wait on the slowest link. Pipeline parallelism across nodes is used only when a model cannot\n   fit in one node.\n2. **Model pool.** All replicas of one model (and one configuration: quantization, max context,\n   adapters). Scaling happens per pool, because a 7B chat model and a 70B reasoning model have\n   completely different capacity curves and traffic.\n3. **Router / gateway.** Receives requests, picks a pool by model name, picks a replica inside the\n   pool, streams tokens back. This is where LLM-aware routing lives.\n4. **Scheduler inside the engine.** Continuous batching admits new sequences into the running\n   batch each step as others finish; it is the reason throughput grows with concurrency until the\n   KV cache is full.\n5. **Orchestrator.** Kubernetes (often with KubeRay or a serving layer such as KServe) places\n   replicas on nodes with the right GPU type and count, restarts failures, and runs the autoscaler.\n\n### Routing that knows what a request costs\nA good router does not round-robin. The usual signals, in rough order of value:\n- **Queue depth and in-flight sequences per replica** (\"least-loaded\"), so a long request does not\n  land on a replica that is already at its concurrency cap. Replicas expose these as metrics\n  (vLLM publishes running/waiting request counts and KV-cache usage).\n- **Prefix-cache affinity.** Requests sharing a long system prompt or a conversation are hashed to\n  the replica that already holds that prefix, which turns a full prefill into a cache hit. This is\n  consistent hashing on the prompt prefix, with spillover to least-loaded when the preferred replica\n  is saturated *because* affinity is worth little if it adds queueing delay.\n- **Session stickiness for multi-turn chats**, a special case of the above.\n- **Priority classes**, so interactive traffic is admitted ahead of batch jobs that share the pool.\n\n### Disaggregated prefill and decode\nPrefill is compute-bound (big matrix multiplies over the whole prompt); decode is bandwidth-bound\n(one token per step, reading the whole cache). Running both on the same replica makes a 40k-token\nprefill stall every decode stream on that GPU for hundreds of milliseconds, which shows up as TPOT\njitter. Larger clusters therefore split them: prefill replicas compute the KV cache and ship it over\na fast interconnect to decode replicas, each class sized and scaled separately. The cost is the KV\ntransfer and more moving parts, so it pays only at scale with long prompts.\n\n### Scaling on the right signal\nThe autoscaler should read engine metrics: waiting-queue length, KV-cache utilization, TTFT, or\ntokens/second against the replica's measured capacity. CPU is meaningless and even GPU utilization\nis misleading, since a replica with its KV cache full of idle-but-live sessions is \"available\" by\nutilization and \"full\" by admission. Scale-up is slow (pulling tens of GB of weights and warming\nthe engine can take minutes), so the cluster needs headroom and predictive or scheduled scaling\nrather than purely reactive rules; scale-down must drain in-flight streams before killing a pod.\n\n### Node topology and placement\nReplicas request whole GPUs (or MIG slices for small models) and must be pinned to node pools that\nhave the right card, because a 70B replica scheduled onto an L4 node simply fails. Spreading\nreplicas of a pool across nodes and zones limits blast radius from a node failure; packing them\nraises the chance that a single node loss removes a whole pool. Both are deliberate choices.","example":"Sizing a pool and routing inside it. The capacity numbers are illustrative placeholders you would\nreplace with your own load-test results.\n\n```python\nimport math, hashlib\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Replica:\n    name: str\n    max_seqs: int            # concurrency at which p95 TPOT still meets the budget (load-tested)\n    running: int = 0\n    hot_prefixes: set = field(default_factory=set)\n\ndef replicas_needed(peak_out_tok_s, out_tok_s_per_replica, headroom=1.3, n_plus=1):\n    \"\"\"Pool size from peak decode demand, with headroom for burst and N+1 for a node loss.\"\"\"\n    return math.ceil(peak_out_tok_s * headroom / out_tok_s_per_replica) + n_plus\n\n# Peak 6,000 output tok/s; a 2xH100 TP=2 replica sustains ~1,500 tok/s within budget (illustrative).\nprint(\"replicas:\", replicas_needed(6_000, 1_500))      # -> ceil(5.2)+1 = 7\n\npool = [Replica(f\"r{i}\", max_seqs=48) for i in range(7)]\n\ndef route(prompt: str, prefix_chars: int = 2048) -> Replica:\n    key = hashlib.sha1(prompt[:prefix_chars].encode()).hexdigest()\n    preferred = pool[int(key, 16) % len(pool)]          # prefix affinity via consistent choice\n    if preferred.running < preferred.max_seqs:\n        preferred.hot_prefixes.add(key); preferred.running += 1\n        return preferred\n    # Affinity is not worth queueing for: spill to the least-loaded replica with room.\n    cands = [r for r in pool if r.running < r.max_seqs]\n    if not cands:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"pool saturated: shed or queue at the gateway\")\n    best = min(cands, key=lambda r: r.running); best.running += 1\n    return best\n```\n\nThe sizing function makes the two hidden assumptions explicit: a replica's capacity is the\nconcurrency at which it still meets the latency budget (not its raw maximum), and one extra replica\ncovers a node loss without breaching it. The router prefers the replica that already holds the\nprompt prefix, but only while it has admission room.","tradeoffs":["Many small replicas (TP=1) give fine-grained scaling and isolation; a few large TP replicas give higher per-replica throughput and are mandatory once weights exceed one GPU, but a single failure removes a bigger slice of capacity.","Prefix-affinity routing cuts prefill cost for shared prompts, but it concentrates load; when one tenant's prefix is hot, you must spill to other replicas or that replica becomes the bottleneck.","Disaggregating prefill from decode stabilises TPOT and lets each phase scale on its own signal, at the price of KV-cache transfer and a second pool to operate; below a few replicas it is not worth it.","Headroom (idle capacity) buys tail-latency protection and absorbs slow scale-up; every point of headroom is paid GPU time, so the right level depends on how spiky traffic is and how long a cold replica takes to become ready.","Spreading replicas across zones improves availability but costs cross-zone traffic and sometimes loses access to the cheapest GPU capacity pool, which flips the decision for batch workloads."],"pitfalls":["Autoscaling on CPU or GPU utilization; the symptom is replicas reporting \"healthy\" and under 50% utilization while TTFT climbs into seconds because the KV cache or waiting queue is full.","Round-robin routing across replicas; the symptom is p99 latency far above p50 with no correlation to overall load, caused by long requests piling onto already-busy replicas.","Killing pods on scale-down without draining; the symptom is a burst of truncated streams and client retries every time the autoscaler steps down.","Treating a tensor-parallel replica as N independent GPUs in the capacity model; the symptom is a plan that promises N× the throughput a TP group actually delivers.","Scheduling a replica onto a node pool with the wrong GPU type or too little host RAM for weight loading; the symptom is CrashLoopBackOff with OOM during model load rather than at serving time.","No readiness gate that waits for the engine's warm-up; the symptom is a wave of errors and timeouts right after every scale-up or rollout because traffic hits a replica still loading weights."],"when":"You need a cluster the moment one replica cannot meet peak demand within the latency budget, when\nyou need to survive a node loss without an outage, or when you serve more than one model. Below\nthat, one well-tuned replica (or a hosted API) is simpler and cheaper. When you do build one, decide\nthe replica shape per model (TP degree, quantization, max context), the routing policy (least-loaded\nfirst, prefix affinity second), the scaling signal (queue depth and KV utilization, never CPU), and\nthe headroom. Measure a replica's capacity curve under load before writing any autoscaling rule, and\nkeep TTFT, TPOT, queue depth and KV usage on one dashboard. In interviews, expect to be asked why\nyou would not use the normal load balancer and autoscaler, and to reason from KV-cache memory and\nprefill/decode asymmetry to the answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures the engine (TP degree, max sequences, max context), exposes its metrics, writes the router's selection logic and the autoscaler's queries, and load-tests each replica shape.","architect":"Decides pool boundaries per model, TP versus more replicas, whether to disaggregate prefill and decode, zone spreading, and the headroom policy that balances cost against tail latency.","fde":"Sizes a customer's cluster from their traffic shape and GPU inventory, explains why their existing Kubernetes autoscaler misbehaves with LLM pods, and sets up the right metrics and drain behaviour."},"check":{"q":"A team runs eight identical vLLM replicas behind an ordinary round-robin load balancer with\nCPU-based autoscaling. Under load, p50 latency is fine but p99 TTFT is 20× p50 and the autoscaler\nnever fires. Diagnose the cause and describe the fix, including what you would measure.","a":"Round-robin ignores that LLM requests vary 100× in length and occupy a replica for seconds to\nminutes, so long requests pile onto replicas that are already at their concurrency cap; those\nreplicas queue new arrivals, which is the p99 tail. CPU never rises because decode is GPU-bandwidth\nbound and the pods are waiting on the GPU, so a CPU rule sees an idle fleet. The fix is to route on\nengine state (least in-flight sequences, with prefix affinity when a prefix is hot) and to scale on\nengine metrics: waiting-queue length and KV-cache utilization, with a TTFT SLO as the alarm. Before\nchanging rules, load-test one replica to find the concurrency at which p95 TPOT still meets budget\nand treat that as its capacity; size the pool from peak tokens/second with headroom and one spare\nreplica, and add a readiness gate so new replicas take traffic only after warm-up."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is tensor parallelism normally kept inside a single node rather than spread across nodes?","options":["Kubernetes cannot schedule a pod across two nodes","It needs an all-reduce every layer for every token, which is only fast enough over intra-node interconnects","Cross-node tensor parallelism would duplicate the model weights on every node","The KV cache must be contiguous in memory, which a network cannot provide"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tensor parallelism slices each layer and exchanges partial results per layer per step; over a network that round-trip would dominate every decode step. Pipeline parallelism is the cross-node option because it exchanges activations only at stage boundaries."},{"q":"Which signal is the best basis for autoscaling an LLM model pool?","options":["Average CPU utilization of the serving pods","GPU SM utilization reported by the driver","Engine queue depth and KV-cache utilization measured against the replica's load-tested capacity","Number of HTTP connections open on the load balancer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Admission is limited by KV-cache memory and the engine's scheduler, not by CPU; GPU utilization can be low while the cache is full of live sessions. Queue depth and cache usage reflect whether the replica can admit more work."},{"q":"Prefix-affinity routing improves cost; under what condition should the router ignore it?","options":["When the prompt is shorter than the model's context window","When the replica holding the prefix is in a different availability zone","When the preferred replica is at its concurrency cap, so spilling to a less-loaded replica avoids queueing delay","When the request uses streaming, since streamed responses cannot reuse a cache"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cache hit saves prefill compute, but sending a request to a saturated replica trades that saving for queueing time and tail-latency damage. Affinity should be a preference with a spillover rule, not a hard constraint."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Sheng et al., 2023 — S-LoRA: Serving Thousands of Concurrent LoRA Adapters","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"A GPU serving an LLM is efficient only when busy. Continuous batching reaches its throughput at\ntens of concurrent sequences; at two or three the GPU spends most of each decode step reading weights\nfor almost no output, so the cost per token can be an order of magnitude higher than at full batch.\nA tenant whose traffic peaks at five concurrent requests cannot fill a replica on their own. Pooling\nmany such tenants fills it, and because their peaks fall at different times, the pool needs capacity\nfor the peak of the sum, which is much less than the sum of the peaks. That is the economic pull\ntoward sharing, and it is strong: for a platform serving dozens of internal teams, dedicated\nreplicas per team can multiply the GPU bill several times for the same total tokens.\n\nThe naive conclusion, \"share everything\", fails the first time one tenant submits a batch job of\n50k-token documents. Those requests fill the KV cache, every other tenant's TTFT jumps, and the\nplatform has no way to say whose fault it is or to stop it, because the engine's scheduler is\nfirst-come-first-served and knows nothing about tenants. It fails again when a regulated tenant asks\nwhether their prompts ever shared a GPU with another company's, or when a tenant needs a different\nmodel, a longer context limit, a fine-tuned adapter or a different region. Sharing without\nisolation mechanisms is a cost optimisation that quietly removes fairness, blast-radius control and\ncontractual guarantees. The decision therefore is which of those properties each tenant actually\nneeds, and the cheapest level of isolation that provides them.","how":"### The isolation spectrum\nFrom cheapest to most isolated, each level buys a specific guarantee:\n\n1. **Shared pool, no tenant awareness.** Maximum utilization, no guarantees. Acceptable only for\n   one team's own traffic or a prototype.\n2. **Shared pool with logical isolation.** Per-tenant rate limits and token budgets at the\n   gateway, priority classes, fair-share admission so one tenant cannot hold more than a share of\n   in-flight sequences, and per-tenant metrics and chargeback. This buys fairness and attribution\n   while keeping the batching economics. It exists *because* the engine scheduler is FCFS; fairness\n   must be enforced before requests reach it.\n3. **Reserved capacity inside a shared cluster.** A tenant gets a guaranteed minimum (their own\n   replicas, or a reserved share of the pool) and may burst into the shared remainder. This is the\n   self-hosted analogue of what hosted providers sell as provisioned throughput next to a\n   pay-as-you-go pool.\n4. **Dedicated replicas or node pool.** Separate engine processes, possibly separate GPUs, so a\n   tenant's KV cache and queue are physically theirs. Buys latency isolation and lets the tenant run\n   a different model, context limit or quantization. Costs idle capacity whenever the tenant is quiet.\n5. **Dedicated cluster, VPC or cloud account.** Separate control plane, network and identity. Buys\n   the strongest compliance story (data residency, no shared control plane, independent blast\n   radius) and the highest operating cost, because every platform component is duplicated.\n\n### What is actually shared at each level\nSharing a *replica* means sharing GPU memory: tenants' KV caches sit in the same pool of blocks,\nand the batch holds their sequences side by side. Within a correct engine, one sequence cannot read\nanother's cache, so this is not a data-leak path in itself; the risks are side-channel timing,\nengine bugs and, more practically, prefix caching: if a cached prefix keyed only by its text could\nbe hit by another tenant's identical text, you have cross-tenant reuse of computation, not data,\nbut it still has to be explicitly decided and keyed. Sharing a *node* but not a replica removes\ncache co-residency and leaves shared host RAM, network and the node's failure domain. Sharing a\n*cluster* leaves shared control plane and shared operators. The compliance question is always \"what\nexactly is shared\", and the answer should be precise, because \"dedicated\" is sold at every level.\n\n### Multi-LoRA: sharing the base, isolating the behaviour\nWhen tenants need customised models, the expensive choice is a dedicated replica per fine-tune. The\ncheaper design keeps one shared base model and loads each tenant's LoRA adapter into the same\nengine; the adapters are small, swapped per request, and the base weights are shared. This gives\nbehavioural isolation (each tenant's outputs come from their adapter) with shared economics. The\nlimit is that all tenants must agree on the base model, context limit and quantization.\n\n### Logical isolation mechanics\nAt the gateway: per-tenant token-bucket limits in requests and tokens per minute, a concurrency cap\n(in-flight sequences per tenant), and a priority tag. At the pool: weighted fair queueing across\ntenants before admission, so a tenant with a backlog of batch work cannot starve interactive users,\nand the option to route a tenant's batch traffic to a separate low-priority pool. In observability:\nevery request carries a tenant id into metrics and traces so cost and tail latency can be attributed.\nWithout attribution you cannot even argue about whether a tenant deserves dedicated capacity.\n\n### How the decision is made\nScore each tenant on four questions. Does their traffic shape threaten others (long contexts,\nbursty batch jobs)? Do they need guarantees others would violate (latency SLO, data separation,\nregion)? Do they need a different model configuration? Would their dedicated capacity sit idle most\nof the day? Tenants with \"yes\" on the first three and \"no\" on the last get dedicated capacity; the\nrest live in the shared pool behind logical isolation. The platform ends up with a shared pool plus\na short list of exceptions, each with a documented reason.","example":"A worked comparison for a platform with ten internal product teams on one 70B model. Numbers are\nillustrative and come from the kind of measurements you would make yourself.\n\nEach team peaks at 20 concurrent sequences, but the peaks are spread over the day; the measured\npeak of the combined traffic is 80 concurrent sequences, not 200. A replica holds 48 concurrent\nsequences within the latency budget.\n\n- **Isolated per team:** every team needs its own replica, since no team's traffic can be combined\n  with another's. 10 replicas, each averaging perhaps 6 of 48 slots busy over the day.\n  Utilization near 12%.\n- **Shared with logical isolation:** peak of the sum is 80, so ceil(80 / 48) = 2 replicas, plus\n  one for N+1 and headroom gives 3. Average utilization is several times higher, and the bill is\n  about 30% of the isolated design for the same tokens.\n- **Hybrid:** one team runs nightly document batches with 60k-token prompts. In the shared pool\n  those jobs would fill the KV cache during the batch window. Give that team a dedicated replica\n  for batch (or route their batch traffic to a low-priority pool), keep their interactive traffic\n  in the shared pool. Total: 4 replicas, fairness preserved, the regulated team that asked for\n  \"no shared GPU\" is still not satisfied and needs level 4 or 5 with a cost line attached.\n\nThe arithmetic that matters is peak-of-sum versus sum-of-peaks, and whether a tenant's shape\nbreaks the shared pool. Whenever the second is true, the first stops mattering for that tenant.","tradeoffs":["Sharing raises utilization and lowers cost per token, but it removes latency isolation; the balance flips when one tenant's traffic shape (long prompts, batch bursts) dominates the pool.","Logical isolation at the gateway preserves batching economics and gives fairness, but it cannot prevent cache contention once requests are admitted, so it does not satisfy hard latency SLOs.","Dedicated replicas give predictable latency and freedom to change model configuration, but capacity sits idle outside the tenant's peak; it flips to worthwhile when the tenant's own traffic can fill a replica most of the day.","Multi-LoRA sharing lets many customised models ride one base at shared cost, but constrains every tenant to the same base model, context and quantization, which breaks when one needs a different model.","Dedicated clusters or accounts give the strongest compliance story, yet duplicate every platform component and operator burden; they are justified by contract or regulation, rarely by performance."],"pitfalls":["Promising \"dedicated\" without stating the level; the symptom is a security review discovering the tenant's \"dedicated\" model shares a node, control plane or prefix cache with others.","Running a shared pool without per-tenant concurrency caps; the symptom is platform-wide TTFT spikes that line up with one tenant's cron schedule.","Keying a shared prefix cache on prompt text alone and not deciding whether cross-tenant hits are acceptable; the symptom is an auditor asking why tenant B's request was faster because tenant A had sent the same prompt.","No tenant id in metrics; the symptom is a cost-allocation argument nobody can settle and an isolation decision made on anecdotes instead of load shape.","Giving every tenant who asks their own replica; the symptom is a fleet averaging single-digit utilization and a GPU bill that scales with the number of customers rather than with tokens.","Treating isolation as permanent; the symptom is a tenant that outgrew or shrank below the threshold still paying (or still being subsidised) a year later."],"when":"Start shared with logical isolation: gateway limits, fair-share admission, per-tenant metrics. That\nis the default for internal platforms and for SaaS tiers without hard SLOs. Move a tenant to\nreserved or dedicated capacity when their traffic shape measurably damages others, when a contract\nspecifies latency or data-separation guarantees you cannot meet in the pool, or when they need a\ndifferent model configuration. Move to a dedicated cluster or account only for regulatory or\ncontractual reasons, and attach the cost to the contract. Measure per-tenant p95 TTFT, in-flight\nsequences and tokens, and pool utilization; revisit each exception quarterly. In interviews, be\nready to explain why continuous batching makes sharing cheap and why that same batching makes\nnoisy neighbours hard to contain after admission.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements tenant-tagged rate limits, concurrency caps and fair-share queueing at the gateway, and ensures tenant ids flow into engine metrics and traces for attribution.","architect":"Owns the isolation policy: which guarantees each tier of tenant gets, at which level of the spectrum, and the cost line that goes with every dedicated exception.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"we need our own model\" into the precise isolation level they need, prices it, and configures reserved or dedicated capacity inside their environment."},"check":{"q":"A SaaS company serves forty customers from one shared vLLM pool. One enterprise customer\ndemands \"dedicated infrastructure\" after a week of slow responses. How do you decide what to give\nthem, and what would you check first?","a":"First check whether the slowness is caused by them or by someone else: per-tenant p95 TTFT\nand in-flight sequences over the week, correlated with pool-wide KV utilization. If another tenant's\nbatch jobs caused it, the fix is logical isolation for everyone (concurrency caps, fair-share\nadmission, a low-priority batch pool), which solves the enterprise customer's problem without\ndedicated hardware. If their own traffic shape is the cause, or their contract names a latency SLO,\ngive them reserved capacity: a dedicated replica for their guaranteed minimum with burst into the\npool. Only if the demand is about data separation or residency do you move to a dedicated node\npool, cluster or account, and then the precise level of what is not shared goes into the contract\nalong with its cost. Dedicated capacity is reviewed against their utilization every quarter."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a shared model pool usually cost far less per token than dedicated replicas per tenant?","options":["Shared pools can use cheaper GPUs because the model is loaded once","Continuous batching only reaches full efficiency at high concurrency, and tenants' peaks rarely coincide, so capacity is sized for the peak of the sum","Dedicated replicas must each keep a separate copy of the training data","Shared pools skip the prefill phase for every tenant after the first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A GPU at low concurrency spends most of each decode step reading weights for little output. Pooling fills the batch and sizes capacity for combined peak rather than the sum of individual peaks, which is where the saving comes from."},{"q":"Per-tenant rate limits and concurrency caps at the gateway are in place, yet one tenant's long-context jobs still slow others. Why?","options":["Rate limits only count requests, never tokens","The gateway cannot see which tenant a request belongs to","Once admitted, sequences share the replica's KV cache and batch, so long contexts consume memory and bandwidth that gateway limits do not govern","Long-context requests bypass the gateway and reach the engine directly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logical isolation controls admission, not what happens inside the batch; long sequences take many KV blocks and lengthen every step. Latency isolation needs separate replicas or routing long jobs to a separate pool."},{"q":"Which situation most justifies a fully dedicated cluster or cloud account rather than dedicated replicas?","options":["The tenant's p95 latency target is tighter than the pool's","The tenant wants a different context length limit","The tenant's contract requires data residency or no shared control plane","The tenant's traffic is the largest in the pool"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Latency and configuration needs are met by dedicated replicas inside a shared cluster. A separate cluster or account is justified by what must not be shared at all: region, control plane, operators and network."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","sources":["Official docs: Amazon Bedrock","Official docs: Azure OpenAI Service","Official docs: Google Cloud Vertex AI"],"why":"Calling a model vendor's public API from an enterprise application raises questions that have\nnothing to do with the model: who approved this vendor, where does the data go, which network path\ndoes the request take, how is it authenticated, where are the logs, who pays and under which\ncontract. Each of those is a review, and each review is slow. Meanwhile the company already has an\nanswer to every one of them for its cloud provider: IAM or Entra identities, private networking,\naudit logging, consolidated billing against a committed spend, and a signed compliance package.\n\nThe cloud AI platforms exist to let the model inherit those answers. A request to Bedrock is a\nSigV4-signed AWS API call over a VPC endpoint, logged to CloudWatch, billed on the AWS invoice. An\nAzure OpenAI deployment is a resource in a resource group with Entra RBAC, a private endpoint and\ndiagnostic settings. A Vertex AI call is a Google Cloud API call under a project's IAM, VPC Service\nControls and Cloud Audit Logs. From the security reviewer's point of view, the model becomes \"just\nanother cloud service\", which is the point.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"it is the same model, use the vendor API and save the complexity\", fails on\nthe review calendar rather than on technology, and later on data terms (training opt-outs, retention,\nresidency) that the cloud contracts fix in writing. It also fails on capacity: committed cloud\ncustomers can buy provisioned throughput in their own region, which a public pay-as-you-go API does\nnot offer in the same form. The opposite naive alternative, \"self-host everything on our GPUs\", fails\nfor most teams on model quality and operations cost, which is why the managed route is the default\nin large organisations.","how":"### The common shape\nAll three platforms present the same architectural layers, and your design decisions live in each:\n\n1. **Identity.** No API keys from the model vendor. Bedrock uses IAM principals and policies per\n   model; Azure OpenAI uses Entra ID (keys exist but are the weaker option); Vertex uses Google\n   Cloud IAM and service accounts. Decision: which workload identities may call which models.\n2. **Network.** Private connectivity so traffic never crosses the public internet: VPC endpoints\n   (PrivateLink) for Bedrock, Private Endpoints for Azure OpenAI, Private Service Connect and VPC\n   Service Controls for Vertex. Decision: where the endpoint sits and which networks can reach it.\n3. **Model catalog and endpoint.** Bedrock offers models from several vendors (Amazon's own,\n   Anthropic, Meta, Mistral and others) behind one API, with the Converse API as the model-agnostic\n   interface. Azure OpenAI hosts OpenAI models, and you create a named *deployment* of a model\n   version with a capacity setting; the wider Azure AI Foundry catalog adds other vendors' models.\n   Vertex serves Gemini natively and partner models (Anthropic, Meta, Mistral and others) through\n   Model Garden. Decision: which models, which versions, pinned how.\n4. **Capacity model.** Two modes everywhere, with different names. Pay-as-you-go shares a regional\n   pool under quotas (requests and tokens per minute per model per region per account or\n   subscription). Reserved capacity buys guaranteed throughput: Bedrock Provisioned Throughput in\n   model units, Azure Provisioned Throughput Units (PTU), Vertex Provisioned Throughput. Reserved\n   capacity exists *because* the shared pool cannot promise throughput at peak; you pay for\n   dedicated model capacity in exchange for predictability. Batch modes exist for offline work at a\n   discount. Decision: which workloads run on shared quota and which need reserved capacity.\n5. **Region.** Models are available per region, and quotas are per region. Each platform has added\n   ways to route across regions when one is full: Bedrock cross-region inference profiles, Azure\n   \"Global\" and \"Data Zone\" deployment types that process within a wider geography, and a global\n   endpoint option on Vertex for some models. These trade residency precision for availability.\n   Decision: which regions satisfy the data-residency rule, and whether cross-region routing is\n   permitted.\n6. **Governance and logging.** Invocation logging to the cloud's log services, content filters\n   (Bedrock Guardrails, Azure's built-in content filtering, Vertex safety settings), and the data\n   terms: all three state that customer prompts and outputs are not used to train the base models.\n   Azure's abuse-monitoring may retain prompts for a limited period unless an exemption is granted,\n   which matters for regulated data and must be checked against current docs.\n7. **Adjacent building blocks.** Managed retrieval (Bedrock Knowledge Bases, Vertex AI Search and\n   RAG tooling, Azure AI Search), agent frameworks, evaluation and fine-tuning services. They are\n   convenient and deepen lock-in; treat each as a separate build-versus-buy decision.\n\n### Where the differences bite\n- **Model lag.** New models and features usually reach the vendor's own API first and the cloud\n  platforms days to months later, sometimes with missing features. If the roadmap depends on the\n  newest model, that lag is a real cost.\n- **Capacity semantics.** Azure's quota is a TPM allocation per model per region per subscription\n  that you split across deployments; Bedrock's on-demand quotas are per model per region per\n  account; Vertex has moved Gemini pay-as-you-go toward a dynamically shared quota rather than\n  fixed per-project limits. The practical consequence is the same: plan capacity per model per\n  region and request increases early, since increases are tickets, not API calls.\n- **SDK shape.** Bedrock has its own SDK (boto3) and the Converse API, plus vendor SDKs that\n  support Bedrock as a backend; Azure OpenAI uses the OpenAI SDK with an Azure client where\n  `model` means the deployment name; Vertex uses the Google Gen AI SDK or vendor SDKs with a Vertex\n  backend. An internal gateway or an abstraction such as LiteLLM usually hides this so that\n  application code is not rewritten when a model moves.\n- **Version pinning and retirement.** Each platform retires model versions on its own schedule;\n  Azure deployments can be set to auto-upgrade or pinned, Bedrock and Vertex pin by model id.\n  Unplanned upgrades change behaviour under your evals, so pinning plus a scheduled re-evaluation\n  is the safe pattern.\n\n### The reference architecture\nApplication → internal AI gateway (auth, routing, quotas, logging, prompt and response capture) →\nprivate endpoint → cloud AI platform in the approved region → optional cross-region or second\nprovider fallback. The gateway is where you keep the option to move between platforms, which is the\nanswer to the second-layer lock-in that these services create.","example":"The same one-turn request through all three platforms. Notice what differs: Bedrock has no key and\nsigns with the IAM identity in the environment; Azure needs an endpoint, an API version and the\n*deployment* name; Vertex authenticates with Application Default Credentials and a project/location.\nModel identifiers are placeholders to look up in each catalog.\n\n```python\nimport os, boto3\nfrom openai import AzureOpenAI\nfrom google import genai\n\nQUESTION = \"Summarise our refund policy in one sentence.\"\n\ndef via_bedrock(model_id: str) -> str:\n    rt = boto3.client(\"bedrock-runtime\", region_name=os.environ[\"AWS_REGION\"])  # IAM creds, SigV4\n    r = rt.converse(\n        modelId=model_id,                                   # or a cross-region inference profile id\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [{\"text\": QUESTION}]}],\n        inferenceConfig={\"maxTokens\": 200},\n    )\n    return r[\"output\"][\"message\"][\"content\"][0][\"text\"]\n\ndef via_azure(deployment_name: str) -> str:\n    client = AzureOpenAI(\n        azure_endpoint=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT\"],   # https://<resource>.openai.azure.com\n        api_key=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY\"],            # prefer Entra ID token auth in prod\n        api_version=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_API_VERSION\"],\n    )\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=deployment_name,                               # deployment, not the model family name\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": QUESTION}], max_tokens=200,\n    )\n    return r.choices[0].message.content\n\ndef via_vertex(model_name: str) -> str:\n    client = genai.Client(vertexai=True, project=os.environ[\"GCP_PROJECT\"],\n                          location=os.environ[\"GCP_LOCATION\"])  # ADC supplies the identity\n    r = client.models.generate_content(model=model_name, contents=QUESTION)\n    return r.text\n```\n\nIn production none of these calls live in application code; they sit behind the gateway, which\ntags each request with tenant, feature and region so that quota use and cost can be attributed and\na fallback can be configured without touching the application.","tradeoffs":["You inherit the cloud's identity, network, logging and compliance perimeter, but you accept model and feature lag behind the vendor's own API; the balance flips when the product depends on the newest model capabilities.","Reserved capacity (PTU, provisioned throughput, model units) buys predictable throughput for peak, but it is paid whether used or not; it is worth it only when steady load fills most of it or an SLO makes shared-pool 429s unacceptable.","Consolidated billing against committed spend simplifies procurement and can discount the model bill, but it ties model choice to one cloud's catalog and makes a multi-cloud model strategy harder.","Managed adjacent services (knowledge bases, agents, guardrails) accelerate delivery, but every one adopted deepens lock-in and narrows the exit path; the trade flips when the component is commodity and replaceable behind an interface.","Cross-region and global routing options raise availability, but they loosen the residency guarantee; where regulation names a region, they must be disabled."],"pitfalls":["Passing the model family name instead of the deployment name to Azure OpenAI; the symptom is a 404 \"deployment not found\" even though the model is clearly available in the region.","Sizing capacity in one region and discovering the model is not offered there or the quota is lower; the symptom is a launch delayed by a quota-increase ticket.","Leaving auto-upgrade or default model versions in place; the symptom is a silent behaviour change that breaks evals or output parsing with no deploy on your side.","Enabling cross-region inference or global deployment types on a workload with a residency requirement; the symptom is an audit finding months later rather than an error at request time.","Calling the platform from application code directly; the symptom is a multi-week rewrite when a model moves clouds or a second provider is added for fallback.","Assuming the cloud's data terms cover abuse-monitoring retention and human review; the symptom is a compliance review rejecting a workload that handles regulated data until the exemption is arranged."],"when":"Reach for a cloud AI platform when the organisation already runs on that cloud, when identity,\nprivate networking and compliance scope are review gates, when spend commitments can absorb the\nmodel bill, or when you need reserved capacity in a chosen region. Prefer the vendor's direct API\nwhen you need the newest models or features first and your data terms are satisfied by the vendor's\ncontract. Prefer self-hosting when open-weight models meet the bar and volume makes it cheaper, or\nwhen the data cannot leave your own hardware. Measure quota headroom per model per region, 429 and\nthrottle rates, and time-to-new-model when a release matters. In interviews, the expected depth is\nexplaining the identity, network, capacity and region layers and how a gateway keeps the exit open.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires the gateway to each platform's SDK, pins model versions, handles each platform's throttling and error shapes, and tags requests for cost and quota attribution.","architect":"Chooses platform versus direct API versus self-host per workload, places endpoints inside the network and identity perimeter, and decides reserved versus shared capacity per region.","fde":"Deploys the solution inside the customer's existing cloud account, works through their quota, region and private-endpoint constraints, and documents which data terms apply."},"check":{"q":"A bank on Azure wants a customer-facing assistant on a frontier model, with EU data residency,\na p95 latency target and a launch in six weeks. Walk through the architecture decisions and the\nrisks you would raise.","a":"Use Azure OpenAI deployed in an EU region, reached through a Private Endpoint from the\napplication's VNet, with Entra ID identities and diagnostic logging to the bank's workspace. Check\nmodel availability in that region first, then request quota early; for the latency target, size\nprovisioned throughput for peak and keep a pay-as-you-go deployment for overflow, or accept 429s\nwith queueing if the SLO allows. Use a regional Standard deployment and avoid Global or Data Zone\ntypes unless legal confirms the geography satisfies residency. Pin the model version and schedule\nre-evaluation before any upgrade. Confirm the abuse-monitoring retention terms and apply for the\nexemption if required. Put all calls behind a gateway so a second region or provider can be added\nas fallback. The biggest risks are quota lead time, model availability in the region, and\nbehaviour drift from version upgrades."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main reason enterprises route model calls through Bedrock, Azure OpenAI or Vertex AI rather than the model vendor's public API?","options":["The cloud platforms serve the models with lower latency","The model inherits the cloud account's identity, private networking, logging, billing and compliance perimeter","The cloud platforms always offer newer model versions","The cloud platforms do not enforce rate limits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The technical model is the same; what changes is that security, procurement and compliance are already solved for the cloud provider. Model versions usually lag, and quotas still apply."},{"q":"Why do all three platforms sell a reserved or provisioned capacity mode alongside pay-as-you-go?","options":["Reserved capacity uses a different, faster model build","Pay-as-you-go cannot be used from a private endpoint","The shared regional pool is governed by quotas and cannot guarantee throughput at peak, so predictability has to be bought as dedicated capacity","Reserved capacity is required for logging and audit features"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shared pools give best-effort throughput under per-region quotas; a workload with a latency SLO at peak needs capacity that is reserved for it, which is what PTU, provisioned throughput and model units provide."},{"q":"A workload with a strict single-country data-residency rule is deployed with a cross-region or global routing option enabled. What is the consequence?","options":["Requests fail immediately with a residency error","Latency improves and nothing else changes","Requests may be processed in another region, violating the rule without any runtime error","The quota is halved because two regions share it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cross-region and global modes are designed to route to wherever capacity exists; they do not know your legal constraints. The violation surfaces in an audit, not in an error, so it must be prevented by configuration."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","sources":["Official docs: OpenAI rate limits","Official docs: Anthropic rate limits","Official docs: Azure OpenAI quotas and limits","Official docs: Amazon Bedrock quotas"],"why":"A provider's GPU fleet is finite and shared by everyone. Without limits, one customer's traffic\nspike would degrade every other customer, and the provider could not size its fleet. Rate limits\nare the provider's admission control; quotas are the allocation of that control per customer. They\nare set per model because models have different cost and capacity, per region because the fleet is\nregional, and per account or subscription because that is the billing and abuse boundary. Tiers\nthat rise with spend exist because payment history is the provider's best signal that a customer is\nreal and predictable.\n\nFrom the application side, the naive model is \"the API is infinite, just call it; retry on error\".\nIt fails in three ways. First, a 429 under sustained demand is not transient: retries add load to a\nlimiter that is already rejecting you, so the retry storm raises latency for every request without\nincreasing throughput. Second, the limit is measured in tokens, not requests, and most providers\nreserve output tokens from `max_tokens` at admission, so a request with `max_tokens=4096` that\nproduces 50 tokens still consumed 4,096 from the minute's budget; a team that never set\n`max_tokens` deliberately can find its effective capacity is a fraction of what the quota implies.\nThird, quotas are regional, and the model the product depends on may not be available, or may have\na lower quota, in the only region the data-residency rule allows. A launch has been delayed by a\nquota ticket more often than by a model's quality. Treating quota as a planned resource like GPU\nmemory, with measurement, headroom and a growth process, is what this topic is about.","how":"### The units\n- **RPM** (requests per minute): the simplest cap, binding for workloads with many small requests.\n- **TPM** (tokens per minute): the cap that usually binds for LLM traffic. Some providers count\n  input and output together; others keep separate input-TPM and output-TPM budgets, which matters\n  because long-prompt RAG traffic and long-output generation traffic exhaust different budgets.\n- **TPD / RPD** (per day): present at lower tiers on some providers, a budget rather than a rate.\n- **Concurrency**: some platforms cap simultaneous in-flight requests rather than, or in addition\n  to, rates.\n- **Per-model, per-region, per-account.** Azure OpenAI allocates TPM per model per region per\n  subscription and you split it across deployments; Bedrock's on-demand quotas are per model per\n  region per account and are raised through the service-quotas process; vendor APIs set limits per\n  organisation per model by usage tier; Vertex has moved toward dynamically shared quota for some\n  models, where the fixed per-project cap gives way to a pool shared across customers.\n\n### How counting works\nProviders implement limits as token buckets or sliding windows: the budget refills continuously, a\nrequest is admitted if the bucket holds enough, otherwise it is rejected with 429 and usually a\n`Retry-After` (or `retry-after-ms`) header saying when to try again. The token cost is estimated at\nadmission from the prompt length plus `max_tokens` (or a default when absent), *because* the\nprovider must reserve capacity before it knows the real output length; several providers then\nadjust the accounting when the response completes. Response headers report the remaining budget,\nfor example OpenAI's `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens` family and Anthropic's\n`anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-remaining` family, which a gateway can read to slow down before the\nfirst 429 rather than after it.\n\n### Quota versus capacity\nA quota is a promise about *your* admission, not about the pool's availability. When the regional\npool is saturated, requests fail with overload or capacity errors (Anthropic's 529, Bedrock's\nthrottling exceptions, Azure 429s on a deployment that is under its TPM) even though you are\nunder quota. Provisioned or reserved capacity exists for this gap: it buys dedicated throughput that\ndoes not compete with the pool, which is why it is priced by the hour rather than by the token.\n\n### Regional limits\nQuotas are per region, which cuts both ways. If residency rules allow, a workload can spread across\nseveral regions or several Azure deployments and multiply its effective quota. If residency names\none region, the quota in that region is the ceiling and the plan must be built around it, including\nlead time for increase requests. Model availability is also regional: the newest model may reach\nsome regions months after others. Cross-region inference profiles, global and data-zone deployment\ntypes and global endpoints are the providers' tools for borrowing capacity from elsewhere; using\nthem is a residency decision as much as a capacity one.\n\n### The client-side design\n1. **Measure demand in the provider's units.** Peak tokens per minute in and out, peak requests\n   per minute, per model, per region, from your own telemetry, with `max_tokens` included in the\n   input-side estimate when the provider reserves it.\n2. **Limit yourself below the quota.** A gateway-level token bucket at roughly 80–90% of each\n   quota smooths bursts, so the provider never sees a storm and the first rejection happens in your\n   queue, where you control priority.\n3. **Queue with priority and bound the wait.** Interactive traffic ahead of batch; shed or defer\n   when the queue exceeds the latency budget rather than letting everything time out.\n4. **Retry correctly.** Honour `Retry-After`, add jitter, cap attempts, and open a circuit breaker\n   when the failure rate shows the pool is overloaded, since retries cannot create capacity.\n5. **Spread and fall back.** Weighted routing across regions, deployments, accounts or providers\n   where allowed, with the fallback order chosen by residency and quality constraints.\n6. **Buy reserved capacity for the base load** and use pay-as-you-go for burst, with batch APIs for\n   offline work because they have separate limits and typically a lower price.\n7. **Operate the quota.** Alert on remaining-budget headers and 429 rates; file increases before\n   growth, not during the incident; keep a table of quota per model per region in the runbook.","example":"A gateway-side limiter that budgets tokens the way the provider does (reserving `max_tokens`),\nroutes across a pool of deployments, honours `Retry-After`, and refuses to retry into an overloaded\npool. Deployment names and quotas are placeholders.\n\n```python\nimport time, random, threading\n\nclass TokenBucket:\n    def __init__(self, per_minute: int, share: float = 0.85):\n        self.cap = per_minute * share; self.tokens = self.cap; self.t = time.time(); self.lock = threading.Lock()\n    def take(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        with self.lock:\n            now = time.time(); self.tokens = min(self.cap, self.tokens + (now - self.t) * self.cap / 60); self.t = now\n            if self.tokens >= n: self.tokens -= n; return True\n            return False\n\nclass Deployment:\n    def __init__(self, name, tpm, rpm):\n        self.name, self.tpm, self.rpm, self.open_until = name, TokenBucket(tpm), TokenBucket(rpm), 0.0\n\npool = [Deployment(\"eu-west-a\", tpm=300_000, rpm=1_800), Deployment(\"eu-west-b\", tpm=150_000, rpm=900)]\n\ndef estimate(prompt_tokens: int, max_tokens: int) -> int:\n    return prompt_tokens + max_tokens          # providers reserve max_tokens at admission\n\ndef call(send, prompt_tokens, max_tokens, attempts=4):\n    need = estimate(prompt_tokens, max_tokens)\n    for attempt in range(attempts):\n        cands = [d for d in pool if d.open_until < time.time() and d.rpm.take(1) and d.tpm.take(need)]\n        if not cands:\n            time.sleep(min(2 ** attempt, 8) * random.uniform(0.5, 1.5)); continue   # wait in our queue\n        d = random.choice(cands)\n        status, headers, body = send(d.name)\n        if status == 200: return body\n        if status == 429:                      # our estimate drifted or the pool is busy: back off\n            time.sleep(float(headers.get(\"retry-after\", 2 ** attempt)) * random.uniform(1.0, 1.5))\n        elif status in (503, 529):             # overloaded: trip this deployment for a while\n            d.open_until = time.time() + 30\n        else:\n            raise RuntimeError(f\"{d.name}: {status}\")\n    raise RuntimeError(\"quota exhausted: shed or defer this request\")\n```\n\nThe limiter admits only when both the request budget and the token budget have room, which is the\ncondition the provider will check; running it at 85% of quota keeps a margin for estimation drift.","tradeoffs":["Self-limiting below quota prevents 429 storms and lets you prioritise, but it leaves a slice of paid-for quota unused; the margin should shrink as your token estimates get better.","Spreading across regions or deployments multiplies quota and availability, but it weakens the residency guarantee and splits prefix-cache and provisioned-capacity benefits across endpoints.","Reserved capacity removes 429 and overload risk for the base load, but it is billed by the hour whether used or not; it pays when steady load fills most of it or an SLO forbids shed traffic.","Setting `max_tokens` tightly frees quota and lowers reservation waste, but a value below what real answers need truncates output; tune it per endpoint from observed output-length percentiles.","Batch APIs give separate limits and a lower price, but hours of turnaround; they flip from useful to useless when a workload is interactive or has a deadline inside the batch window."],"pitfalls":["Retrying 429s immediately and without a cap; the symptom is latency rising across every endpoint while throughput stays flat, because retries are consuming the budget that fresh requests need.","Leaving `max_tokens` at a large default; the symptom is hitting TPM at a fraction of the token volume the quota suggests, visible as 429s with low measured output tokens.","Planning capacity in one region without checking model availability and quota there; the symptom is a launch waiting on a quota-increase ticket.","Treating a quota as a capacity guarantee; the symptom is overload errors during a provider-wide peak while your dashboards show you well under quota.","Ignoring remaining-budget headers; the symptom is learning about exhaustion from the first 429 instead of throttling smoothly before it.","Enabling cross-region or global routing to fix a quota problem on a residency-bound workload; the symptom is an audit finding rather than a runtime error."],"when":"This is relevant to every workload on a hosted model API. Plan quotas when you choose the model and\nregion, not at launch: convert peak demand into the provider's units, compare with the current\nallocation per model per region, and file increases with weeks of lead time. Put a self-limiter,\npriority queue and correct retry logic in the gateway from day one; add spreading across regions or\ndeployments when residency allows and demand exceeds one region's quota; buy reserved capacity when\nthere is a steady base load or an SLO at peak. Measure 429 and overload rates, remaining budget,\nqueue wait time, and actual versus reserved output tokens. In interviews, expect questions on why\nretries do not fix sustained 429s and why `max_tokens` affects throughput before it affects cost.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements token-aware limiting, priority queueing and `Retry-After` handling in the gateway, tunes `max_tokens` per endpoint and surfaces remaining-budget headers as metrics.","architect":"Decides the region, deployment and provider topology per workload, when to buy reserved capacity, and how residency constraints bound the spreading options.","fde":"Audits a customer's quotas per model per region before go-live, drives the increase requests early, and explains overload errors that occur while they are under quota."},"check":{"q":"A team on a hosted API has a quota of 400k TPM for their model in the one region their\nresidency rule allows. Their load test shows 429s starting at about 120k measured tokens per\nminute. Explain what is probably happening and how you would raise effective capacity without\nbreaking the residency rule.","a":"The provider reserves `max_tokens` at admission; if requests set `max_tokens` at 4,000 while\nreal answers average a few hundred tokens, each request reserves far more than it uses, so the\nlimiter sees the quota exhausted at a third of the measured usage. First, set `max_tokens` from\nobserved output-length percentiles per endpoint, which can roughly triple effective capacity on\nits own. Second, add a gateway token bucket at 85% of quota with a priority queue so interactive\ntraffic is admitted first and batch work is deferred rather than retried. Third, move offline work\nto the batch API, which has separate limits. Fourth, file a quota increase with usage evidence,\nand if a peak SLO exists, buy provisioned capacity in the same region for the base load. Spreading\nto other regions is off the table because of residency, so the plan is efficiency, prioritisation\nand reserved capacity in-region."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does retrying immediately after a 429 fail to improve throughput under sustained demand?","options":["429 responses are cached by the provider for one minute","The limiter is already rejecting at the quota, so retries consume budget that new requests need and only add latency","Retries are billed at double the token price","The provider blocks the account after repeated 429s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A 429 under sustained load means the budget is exhausted; another request does not add capacity, it competes for the same refill. Back off with jitter, honour Retry-After, and prioritise in your own queue."},{"q":"Why does an overly large `max_tokens` reduce effective throughput on a tokens-per-minute quota?","options":["Larger outputs always take longer to generate","The provider charges for `max_tokens` whether used or not","Most providers reserve `max_tokens` against the budget at admission, so unused reservation still counts for that minute","Requests with large `max_tokens` are routed to a slower pool"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Billing is on actual tokens, but the rate limiter must reserve before it knows the output length, so it uses `max_tokens`. Setting it from real output-length percentiles recovers the wasted budget."},{"q":"A residency-bound workload exceeds the quota available in its region. Which option is valid?","options":["Enable the provider's global routing so excess traffic is served elsewhere","Spread traffic across accounts in several regions","Improve efficiency, prioritise traffic, use batch APIs for offline work, and request reserved capacity or a quota increase in that region","Disable rate limiting in the client so requests are not delayed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Global or multi-region routing violates residency silently. The in-region toolkit is tighter token reservation, prioritisation, separate batch limits, and reserved capacity or increases with lead time."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","sources":["Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: Kubernetes"],"why":"Three forces push inference on-prem. Regulation and contracts: health, financial, defence and\npublic-sector data often may not be processed outside a named facility, jurisdiction or the\ncustomer's own account, and \"the provider promises not to train on it\" does not satisfy that rule.\nPhysics and connectivity: a factory, ship, hospital or retail site may need inference near the\nmachines generating the data, or have links that are slow, metered or intermittent. Economics: a\nworkload that runs flat-out all day on open-weight models can cost less on amortised owned GPUs\nthan on per-token prices, once utilization is high enough.\n\nTwo forces push the other way. The strongest models are available only as hosted APIs, and most\nproducts want them for at least some tasks. Elasticity: owned GPUs are a fixed quantity bought on a\nprocurement cycle measured in months, so a traffic spike or a new use case cannot be met by\n\"buying more\" this week. On-prem also means owning the operations: drivers, firmware, CUDA\nstacks, hardware failures, spares, power and cooling, security patching of the serving stack.\n\nThe naive answers both fail. \"Everything on-prem\" leaves teams on last year's open-weight model,\nunable to serve a launch spike, and paying a platform team for work the cloud would have done.\n\"Everything in the cloud\" fails the first compliance review of the regulated workload and the\nfirst site with a bad link. Hybrid is the admission that different requests have different\nconstraints and the infrastructure should route accordingly, which turns the question into a\npolicy problem: which request goes where, enforced by a gateway, with the application unaware.","how":"### What \"on-prem\" covers\nThe term spans a spectrum: your own datacenter; colocation space with your hardware; a private\ncloud; a customer's cloud account or VPC (cloud hardware, customer control); cloud-provider\non-premises extensions that place the provider's managed stack in your facility (offerings such as\nAWS Outposts, Azure's on-premises stack and Google Distributed Cloud are examples of this\ncategory); and fully air-gapped sites with no external connectivity at all. The control, cost and\noperational burden differ at each point, so \"on-prem\" in a requirement must be pinned down before\nanything is designed. The deployment module covers the customer-VPC and air-gapped cases in depth\nfrom the forward-deployed perspective; this lesson covers the architecture that spans both sides.\n\n### The routing policy\nEvery request carries attributes the gateway can read: the data classification of its inputs,\nthe latency budget, the model capability required, the tenant and the site. The policy maps those\nto a target:\n\n- **Restricted data** → on-prem pool only, never bursts, no fallback to cloud. The rule is\n  enforced at the gateway *because* enforcing it in each application means every application is\n  a compliance risk.\n- **Internal data, frontier capability needed** → cloud platform in the approved region, through\n  private connectivity.\n- **Public or synthetic data, bulk volume** → on-prem when capacity is free (it is already paid\n  for), cloud burst when the on-prem queue exceeds a wait threshold.\n- **Site-local, low latency or offline** → the site's own small pool, with a degraded mode when\n  the central systems are unreachable.\n\nClassification comes from the data source (document labels, database column tags, tenant\ncontract), not from inspecting the prompt at request time, since inspection is a detector with a\nmiss rate and the rule must hold at the boundary regardless.\n\n### A uniform API surface\nApplication code should not know which side served it. On-prem engines (vLLM, TGI and others)\nexpose OpenAI-compatible endpoints, and hosted platforms are reached through their SDKs; the\ngateway presents one interface and holds the policy, the credentials for each side, the quotas and\nthe telemetry. This is what makes moving a workload from one side to the other a configuration\nchange. The exception is behaviour: an open-weight model on-prem and a frontier model in the cloud\nanswer differently, so prompts and evals are per route, and a workload that may be served by either\nside must pass its eval on both.\n\n### Bursting and backflow\nCloud burst works like an overflow queue: when the on-prem pool's queue wait exceeds a threshold,\neligible requests (eligibility is the classification again) go to the cloud. Running it in the other\ndirection, cloud by default with on-prem absorbing what it can, keeps the owned GPUs busy and is\noften the better default for workloads allowed on both sides, since the fixed cost is sunk and the\ncloud cost is marginal.\n\n### Operating the owned side\nA fixed pool is only cheaper when busy, so on-prem capacity should be sized for the steady base of\neligible work, not for peak; peak goes to the cloud. The owned side needs a hardware lifecycle\n(procurement lead time, spares, replacement cadence, typically a three-to-five-year amortisation\nfor the TCO model), a serving-stack lifecycle (engine upgrades, CVE patching, driver and CUDA\ncompatibility), monitoring unified with the cloud side, and a model lifecycle (which open-weight\nmodels, which quantization, evaluated against which cloud baseline). Connectivity between sides is\nprivate (VPN, dedicated links or private endpoints) and secrets for each side are held only where\nthey are used, so that a compromised on-prem node does not hold cloud credentials and vice versa.\n\n### Economics in one line\nOwned cost per token = (amortised hardware + power + space + people) ÷ tokens actually served, so\nit falls with utilization; hosted cost per token is roughly flat. The crossover utilization is the\nbreak-even, covered in its own lesson; the hybrid pattern's job is to keep the owned side above it\nby giving it all the eligible work.","example":"A gateway routing policy that enforces classification, routes for capability and bursts only\neligible traffic. Thresholds and model names are placeholders.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom enum import Enum\n\nclass DataClass(Enum):\n    RESTRICTED = \"restricted\"   # may never leave the owned environment\n    INTERNAL = \"internal\"       # cloud allowed in the approved region, via private link\n    PUBLIC = \"public\"\n\n@dataclass\nclass Request:\n    data_class: DataClass\n    needs_frontier: bool        # task requires a capability only the hosted model has\n    latency_budget_ms: int\n    site: str | None = None     # set for site-local traffic\n\nONPREM_QUEUE_WAIT_MS = {\"central\": 0, \"plant-7\": 0}   # refreshed from engine metrics\nBURST_THRESHOLD_MS = 800\n\ndef route(r: Request) -> str:\n    if r.data_class is DataClass.RESTRICTED:\n        if r.needs_frontier:\n            raise PermissionError(\"restricted data cannot use the hosted frontier model\")\n        return \"onprem:central\"                          # no burst, no fallback, by policy\n    if r.site and r.latency_budget_ms < 300:\n        return f\"onprem:{r.site}\"                        # site-local pool, degraded mode if central is down\n    if r.needs_frontier:\n        return \"cloud:eu-region\"                         # private endpoint, approved region\n    if ONPREM_QUEUE_WAIT_MS[\"central\"] > BURST_THRESHOLD_MS and r.latency_budget_ms < 5_000:\n        return \"cloud:eu-region\"                         # eligible burst: internal/public data only\n    return \"onprem:central\"                              # owned capacity is sunk cost: keep it busy\n\nprint(route(Request(DataClass.RESTRICTED, False, 2000)))        # onprem:central\nprint(route(Request(DataClass.INTERNAL, True, 2000)))           # cloud:eu-region\nONPREM_QUEUE_WAIT_MS[\"central\"] = 1500\nprint(route(Request(DataClass.PUBLIC, False, 2000)))            # cloud:eu-region (burst)\n```\n\nThe policy refuses rather than degrades when restricted data needs a capability only the cloud\nhas; that refusal is the signal to procure a better on-prem model, not a bug to route around.","tradeoffs":["Owned GPUs lower cost per token at high utilization, but they are a fixed quantity with months of lead time; the trade flips against ownership when load is spiky or still growing fast.","On-prem satisfies residency and air-gap rules, but confines those workloads to open-weight models that typically trail the frontier; when the task needs frontier quality, the choice is a better on-prem model or a policy change, not a routing trick.","Bursting to the cloud absorbs peaks without over-buying hardware, but every burst path is a data path that must be gated by classification, and prompts must be evaluated on both models.","A uniform gateway interface makes migration a configuration change, but it hides behavioural differences between routes, which is why evals and prompts must stay per route.","Cloud-provider on-premises extensions reduce the operations burden of owned hardware, but keep the provider's pricing and lock-in inside your building."],"pitfalls":["Sizing on-prem for peak instead of base load; the symptom is a GPU fleet averaging well under a quarter utilization while the finance model assumed near-full.","Classification by prompt inspection instead of by source; the symptom is a restricted document found in cloud logs because a detector missed it.","One prompt tuned on the cloud model and reused on the on-prem model; the symptom is a quality regression that only appears during bursts or on-prem fallback.","No spare hardware or replacement plan; the symptom is weeks of reduced capacity after a single GPU or node failure.","Cloud credentials stored on on-prem nodes for \"convenience\"; the symptom is a security review finding that an on-prem compromise exposes the cloud account.","Treating a customer VPC deployment as \"on-prem\" in the TCO model; the symptom is a cost estimate that omits the customer's cloud bill and the provider's egress and support terms."],"when":"Reach for hybrid when a named class of data or sites cannot use hosted APIs *and* other workloads\nneed frontier models or elastic capacity. Stay fully hosted when no residency or connectivity\nconstraint exists and volume is below the break-even. Go fully on-prem only when policy forbids\nevery hosted path and open-weight models meet the quality bar. Measure owned-pool utilization,\nburst volume and its cost, per-route quality on shared evals, and the number of policy refusals,\nwhich tells you where the on-prem model is falling short. In day-to-day work this shows up as the\ngateway's policy table and the quarterly review of what each side costs; in interviews, expect to\ndefend why classification is enforced at the gateway and why on-prem is sized for base load.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the gateway policy and burst logic, runs OpenAI-compatible engines on the owned side, and maintains per-route prompts and evals so either side passes the same test suite.","architect":"Defines the data classes and their permitted targets, sizes the owned pool to the base load, owns the TCO and break-even model, and signs off on the connectivity and secrets design.","fde":"Deploys the on-prem or customer-VPC side inside the customer's environment, maps their data classifications to routes, and sets up burst eligibility and monitoring that the customer's security team accepts."},"check":{"q":"A hospital group wants an assistant over patient records (must stay on-site), a general\nstaff assistant, and overnight batch summarisation of public research papers. They own eight\nGPUs. Design the hybrid layout and say what you would measure to know it is working.","a":"Patient-record traffic is restricted: it runs on the owned pool with an open-weight model,\nno burst, no fallback, enforced by the gateway from the record system's classification, and the\nmodel is evaluated on clinical tasks before launch. The staff assistant needs frontier quality and\ntouches internal but not restricted data, so it goes to a cloud platform in an approved region\nover a private endpoint. The research batch is public data: it runs on the owned pool overnight\nwhen the restricted workload is quiet, bursting to the cloud batch API if it cannot finish by\nmorning. Size the owned pool for the restricted base load plus the overnight batch, not for peak.\nMeasure owned-pool utilization (target high, since cost per token depends on it), queue wait and\nburst volume, per-route eval scores, and policy refusals, which show where the on-prem model is\ninsufficient."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should data classification for routing be derived from the data source rather than from inspecting the prompt?","options":["Prompt inspection is too slow for interactive latency budgets","A detector has a miss rate, while source-level labels make the restriction hold at the boundary regardless of prompt content","Prompts are encrypted before they reach the gateway","Source labels are required by the serving engine to allocate KV cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compliance rules are absolute; a classifier that misses one restricted document has already violated them. Labels attached to the record system, tenant or document travel with the request and are enforced deterministically."},{"q":"Which sizing rule keeps owned GPUs economical in a hybrid design?","options":["Size on-prem for peak so the cloud is never needed","Size on-prem for the steady base of eligible work and send peaks to the cloud","Size on-prem for the restricted workload only and send everything else to the cloud","Size on-prem to match the cloud quota in the approved region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Owned cost per token falls with utilization, so the pool must be busy; sizing for base load keeps it full while elastic cloud capacity absorbs the peaks that would otherwise sit idle on owned hardware."},{"q":"A restricted-data workload needs a capability only the hosted frontier model has. What should the gateway do?","options":["Route to the cloud once with logging so the exception is auditable","Strip identifying fields from the prompt and route to the cloud","Refuse the request and surface it as a signal to improve the on-prem model or change policy","Route to the cloud only during off-peak hours when scrutiny is lower"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The restriction is on the data, not on the time or on identifiers that a detector might miss. The correct response is a policy refusal, which becomes evidence for procuring a stronger on-prem model or formally revisiting the rule."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment","sources":["Frantar et al., 2022 — GPTQ: Accurate Post-Training Quantization for Generative Pre-trained Transformers","Official docs: llama.cpp","Official docs: ONNX Runtime"],"why":"A cloud call costs a network round trip, needs connectivity, uploads the user's data and bills per\ntoken. For many workloads none of that matters. For some it is disqualifying: a keyboard\nsuggestion or voice assistant that waits 400 ms for a server feels broken; a field technician in a\nbasement has no signal; a camera stream from a factory line cannot be shipped to a datacenter for\nevery frame; a health app's users will not accept their data leaving the phone; and a product with\na hundred million installs cannot pay per-token prices for every interaction. Each of those is a\nreason to run the model where the data is.\n\nThe naive answer, \"ship the model we run in the cloud\", fails on arithmetic. A 70B-parameter model\nin fp16 is roughly 140 GB of weights; a phone gives an app a few gigabytes at most, and even a\nlaptop with 16 GB of unified memory can hold only a quantized 7–8B model with room for the OS and\nthe KV cache. Decode speed is bound by memory bandwidth, which on a phone is a small fraction of a\ndatacenter GPU's, so the same model that streams at 100 tokens per second in the cloud would crawl\non device even if it fit. Thermal limits add a third constraint: sustained inference throttles the\nchip and drains the battery. The opposite naive answer, \"quantize everything to 4-bit and hope\",\nfails on quality: compression without evaluation produces models that pass a demo and fail in the\nfield. Edge deployment is therefore a budgeting and engineering discipline, not a flag on the cloud\nmodel.","how":"### Start from the device budget\nThree numbers bound the design. **Memory**: weights plus KV cache plus activations must fit in what\nthe platform grants an app, with margin for the app itself. Weight bytes ≈ parameters × bits per\nweight ÷ 8, so a 3B model at 4-bit is about 1.5 GB and an 8B model about 4 GB, before the cache.\n**Bandwidth**: each decode step reads all the weights once, so tokens per second is bounded by\nmemory bandwidth ÷ model bytes; as an illustrative order of magnitude, a device with tens of GB/s\nof memory bandwidth and a 2 GB model cannot exceed a few tens of tokens per second, whatever the\ncompute. **Power and thermal**: sustained throughput is lower than burst throughput because the\nchip throttles, so plan for sustained figures measured on warm devices.\n\n### Make the model fit\n- **Choose a small model first.** Small language models (hundreds of millions to a few billion\n  parameters) designed for the task beat a crushed large model at the same byte budget, because\n  quality lost to aggressive quantization is not recovered by size.\n- **Quantize**, typically to 4-bit or 8-bit weights, with activations kept at higher precision.\n  Post-training quantization methods (GPTQ, AWQ and the k-quant families used by llama.cpp) cut\n  memory and raise bandwidth-bound speed roughly in proportion to the bit reduction. The quality\n  cost must be measured per model and task; it is small for many models at 8-bit and 4-bit but\n  can be severe for the smallest models.\n- **Distil** a large model's behaviour into a small one for the specific task, which is how most\n  production on-device models are made task-competent.\n- **Prune and adapt.** Structured pruning removes capacity the task does not need; LoRA adapters\n  let one base model serve several device features with small per-feature deltas.\n\n### Match the runtime to the silicon\nDevice accelerators are heterogeneous: CPU vector units, mobile GPUs, NPUs on phones and laptops,\nApple's unified-memory GPU and Neural Engine, NVIDIA Jetson-class embedded GPUs. Runtimes exist to\nmap one model to many of them: llama.cpp with its GGUF format (CPU and GPU back ends across\ndesktop and mobile), ONNX Runtime (execution providers per accelerator, including mobile), Core ML\n(Apple), LiteRT (formerly TensorFlow Lite) and ExecuTorch (mobile and embedded from the TensorFlow\nand PyTorch ecosystems), TensorRT (NVIDIA embedded and desktop GPUs), and WebGPU-based runtimes\nfor in-browser inference. The runtime decision is made *per device class* because the fastest\npath on each is different, and the test matrix must cover real devices, not a developer laptop.\n\n### Hybrid by default\nA small on-device model handles what it can; requests that need more capability, more context or\nmore knowledge escalate to the cloud. The escalation rule is explicit (confidence threshold, task\ntype, user setting) and is itself a privacy decision, since escalation uploads data. On-device\nfirst with cloud fallback is the shape most consumer platforms have converged on, and it preserves\nthe latency and offline benefits for the common case.\n\n### Lifecycle on a fleet you do not control\nModel files are distributed like app assets: downloaded on Wi-Fi, versioned, delta-updated, with\nintegrity checks. A device may have any of several model versions at once, so telemetry (opt-in,\naggregated) must carry the model version, and the server-side fallback must accept requests from\nold clients. Capability detection at install time decides which model variant, if any, a device\ngets; low-end devices get cloud-only mode. Rollouts are staged by device class because a model\nthat fits one phone can crash another.\n\n### Security on device\nAnything on the device belongs to the user, including the weights, the prompts and any guardrail\nlogic. Treat model weights as extractable, keep anything proprietary in the cloud model, and never\nrely on on-device prompts or filters for decisions that matter to the server; re-check on the\nserver side when the request escalates.","example":"Running a quantized GGUF model locally with llama.cpp through its Python binding, with a budget\ncheck first. The model file name is a placeholder; parameters and quantization are illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport os, time\nfrom llama_cpp import Llama\n\ndef fits(model_path: str, n_ctx: int, kv_bytes_per_token: int, budget_bytes: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Weights on disk approximate weights in memory for GGUF; add the KV cache for n_ctx.\"\"\"\n    weights = os.path.getsize(model_path)\n    need = weights + n_ctx * kv_bytes_per_token\n    print(f\"weights {weights/1e9:.2f} GB + cache {n_ctx*kv_bytes_per_token/1e9:.2f} GB vs budget {budget_bytes/1e9:.1f} GB\")\n    return need <= budget_bytes\n\nMODEL = \"small-chat-3b.Q4_K_M.gguf\"          # ~3B params at 4-bit: roughly 1.5-2 GB on disk\nN_CTX = 2048\n# kv_bytes_per_token = 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes; read these from the model card\nassert fits(MODEL, N_CTX, kv_bytes_per_token=2 * 28 * 8 * 128 * 2, budget_bytes=4 * 1024**3)\n\nllm = Llama(model_path=MODEL, n_ctx=N_CTX, n_threads=os.cpu_count() or 4,\n            n_gpu_layers=-1, verbose=False)   # offload all layers to Metal/CUDA/Vulkan if present\n\nt = time.time()\nout = llm.create_chat_completion(\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Reply in one sentence.\"},\n              {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise why the pump alarm fired.\"}],\n    max_tokens=64,\n)\ntext = out[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"]\nn_out = out[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"]\nprint(text); print(f\"{n_out/(time.time()-t):.1f} tok/s on this device\")\n# Measure sustained tok/s after several minutes on a warm device, not this first burst.\n```\n\nThe `fits` check is the on-device version of the capacity question: weights plus cache against\nthe budget the platform grants. The printed tokens-per-second figure is the number that decides\nwhether streaming feels responsive, and it will drop as the device heats up.","tradeoffs":["On-device inference removes network latency, cloud cost and data upload, but caps model quality at what fits in the device's memory and bandwidth; the trade flips when the task needs frontier capability or broad knowledge.","Quantization to 4-bit roughly halves memory and raises speed versus 8-bit, but the quality loss grows as models get smaller; measure on the task before shipping.","A hybrid escalation path recovers quality for hard cases, but every escalation is a network dependency and a privacy event, so the rule must be visible to the user and the product.","Per-device runtimes extract the most from each accelerator, but multiply the build, test and update matrix; a single portable runtime is slower and simpler.","Owning the fleet's compute makes marginal cost near zero, but makes rollouts, debugging and telemetry slower and partial compared with a server you control."],"pitfalls":["Benchmarking on a developer laptop; the symptom is a model that is fast in the demo and crawls or crashes on the median user's device.","Measuring burst throughput; the symptom is responsiveness collapsing after a minute of use as the device throttles thermally.","Forgetting the KV cache in the memory budget; the symptom is out-of-memory kills on long conversations even though the weights \"fit\".","Shipping a quantized model without task evals; the symptom is a quality regression reported from the field that the cloud model never showed.","Relying on on-device guardrails or prompts for policy; the symptom is users bypassing them by editing the app or its model files.","No model-version field in telemetry and requests; the symptom is a bug that reproduces on some devices and cannot be attributed to the variant they run."],"when":"Reach for edge deployment when latency must be below a network round trip, when the device is\noften offline, when the data must not leave it, or when per-interaction cloud cost at your install\nbase is untenable, and when a small or distilled model can meet the quality bar on the task. Do not\nreach for it when the task needs frontier reasoning or large context, or when the device matrix is\ntoo fragmented to test. Measure sustained tokens per second on target devices, memory high-water\nmarks with the KV cache, task-eval scores per quantization level, escalation rate to the cloud, and\ncrash rates by device class. In interviews, expect to size a model to a device from parameter\ncount and bit width and to explain why decode speed follows memory bandwidth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Quantizes and evaluates candidate models, integrates the runtime for each target platform, implements the memory check and the escalation path, and builds the device test matrix.","architect":"Sets the on-device versus cloud split per feature, the privacy rule for escalation, the model distribution and versioning scheme, and which capabilities stay server-side for security.","fde":"Fits a customer's workload to their actual devices (plant controllers, handhelds, kiosks), proves sustained performance on site, and designs the offline and degraded modes they need."},"check":{"q":"A logistics company wants a package-scanning assistant on handheld devices with 6 GB of RAM,\nof which the app may use about 3 GB, working in warehouses with poor connectivity. Walk through\nhow you would choose and validate the model.","a":"Budget first: 3 GB must hold weights, the KV cache for the longest expected interaction and\nthe app. A 3B-class model at 4-bit is about 1.5 GB of weights, leaving room for a 2k-token cache\nand the app; an 8B model at 4-bit (about 4 GB) does not fit. Then quality: distil or fine-tune the\nsmall model on the scanning tasks and evaluate it against the cloud model on a task set, at the\nexact quantization that will ship. Then speed: measure sustained tokens per second on the real\nhandheld after thermal steady state, since decode is bound by its memory bandwidth, and check the\nhigh-water memory mark on long sessions. Design offline-first with queued escalation to the cloud\nwhen connectivity returns for cases below a confidence threshold. Stage the rollout by device\nclass, carry the model version in telemetry, and keep any policy enforcement on the server."},"quiz":[{"q":"What primarily bounds decode tokens per second for an LLM on a phone?","options":["The number of CPU cores available to the app","Memory bandwidth divided by the bytes of weights read per step","The size of the KV cache in the longest conversation","Network latency to the model's update server"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each generated token requires reading all the weights once; a phone's memory bandwidth is a small fraction of a datacenter GPU's, so the model's byte size divided into that bandwidth gives the ceiling. Quantization helps precisely because it shrinks the bytes read."},{"q":"Why is a purpose-built small model usually better on device than a large model quantized aggressively to fit?","options":["Small models do not need a KV cache","Large models cannot be quantized below 8-bit","Quality lost to extreme quantization is not recovered by parameter count, while a small model trained or distilled for the task keeps its quality at the same byte budget","Small models run on the CPU, which is always faster than the NPU"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At a fixed memory budget the question is where quality per byte is highest. Pushing a large model to very low bit widths degrades it sharply, whereas a distilled small model at 4-bit or 8-bit keeps task performance."},{"q":"Which safeguard belongs on the server even when the model runs entirely on device?","options":["The tokenizer, so that vocabulary stays consistent","The model's quantization tables","Policy enforcement for decisions that matter, since on-device prompts, filters and weights can be modified by the user","The KV cache, to save device memory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Everything on the device is under the user's control and can be extracted or altered. Guardrails that protect the business or other users must be re-checked server-side when requests escalate or produce consequential actions."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","sources":["Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance"],"why":"The usual history: a prototype is built on the largest model with a long, example-stuffed prompt. It works,\nit ships, and then three things happen at once. The bill grows linearly with traffic; p95 latency of several\nseconds makes the interface feel broken; and when someone finally runs an eval, the \"best\" model turns out\nto be no better on this task than one an order of magnitude cheaper, because the task saturates.\n\nThe naive strategies each fail on one axis. \"Use the best model for everything\" ignores that quality is\ntask-relative and plateaus, while cost and latency are paid on every single request. \"Use the cheapest\nmodel\" ignores that a wrong answer has a cost too: a retry, a human escalation, a lost customer. The right\ndenominator is cost per *successful* task, and on that measure the cheap model can be the expensive one.\n\"Tune one axis at a time\" is the most common failure: a team trims the prompt to cut cost, quality quietly\ndrops, and nobody notices until a customer does, because quality was never a number on a dashboard.\n\nThe axes are coupled by mechanism, not by accident. More reasoning tokens means better answers on hard\nproblems and more decode time and spend. More retrieved context means more grounding and more prefill. A\nbigger model means more capability and more memory bandwidth per token. You therefore need a method that\nholds all three in view, makes the exchange rate between them explicit, and is re-run whenever a model,\nprice or prompt changes.","how":"### Make all three measurable\nNothing below works without numbers for each axis on the *same* set of requests.\n\n- **Quality** is a task-specific eval: a golden set with a pass criterion (exact match for classification,\n  a rubric or LLM judge for open text, human grading for the ambiguous slice). It is defined first\n  *because* every cost or latency cut is a bet that quality survives, and bets need a scoreboard.\n- **Latency** must be the latency the user feels: time-to-first-token for streamed chat, end-to-end time\n  for a classifier or a tool call, and always a percentile (p95 or p99), not a mean. Means hide the tail,\n  and the tail is what users remember.\n- **Cost** is cost per successful task, including retries, fallbacks, judge or verifier calls, cache\n  misses and the downstream cost of failures. Per-request cost is what the provider bills; per-successful\n  task is what the business pays.\n\n### Know which axis each lever moves\n| Lever | Quality | Latency | Cost | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bigger model tier | up | up | up | coarsest lever; quality gain saturates per task |\n| Shorter prompt / less context | risk | down (prefill) | down | every token is paid on every call |\n| Capped or terser output | neutral if instructed | down (decode) | down | decode is sequential, so output length dominates total time |\n| Reasoning / thinking budget | up on hard tasks | up a lot | up a lot | wasted on easy tasks; gate by difficulty |\n| Prompt caching (shared prefix) | none | down (TTFT) | down (input) | removes work instead of trading it |\n| Response / semantic cache | none on hits | near zero on hits | near zero on hits | only for repeat queries; staleness risk |\n| Routing / cascade | ≈ large model | up for escalated requests | down on average | depends on escalation rate |\n| Batch / async endpoints | none | up (minutes to hours) | down sharply | for non-interactive work only |\n| Fine-tune / distill small model | up for small model | down | down | front-loaded effort; shifts the frontier |\n| Quantization, speculative decoding | small risk | down | down | serving-side; measure per model |\n| Streaming | none | perceived down | none | changes perception, not the number |\n\nTwo groups matter. Most levers move you *along* the frontier: you give up some of one axis for another.\nA few, caching, distillation, fine-tuning and task redesign, *shift* the frontier, because they remove work\nor make a cheaper model genuinely better at this task. When no point on the current frontier satisfies the\nconstraints, only the second group can help; more tuning of the first group cannot.\n\n### Build the frontier\nRun every candidate configuration against the same eval set and a replay of real traffic, and record the\ntriple (quality, p95 latency, cost per successful task). Configuration A is *dominated* if some B is at\nleast as good on all three and strictly better on one; discard the dominated ones. What remains is the\nPareto frontier. The point of the exercise is that it turns a three-way argument into a short list and makes\nthe exchange rate visible: \"moving from B to C buys two points of quality for $0.003 per task and 450 ms\nof p95\". People can argue about whether that trade is worth it; they can no longer argue about facts.\n\n### Choose under constraints\nIn practice two axes are constraints and one is the objective. Either *maximize quality subject to\np95 ≤ L and cost ≤ C*, or *minimize cost subject to quality ≥ Q and p95 ≤ L*. The quality floor is nearly\nalways a product requirement rather than a tunable, so the second form is the common one. Pick the\nfeasible frontier point with the best objective. If the feasible set is empty, stop tuning and reach for a\nfrontier-shifting lever.\n\n### Do it per task, not per application\nA single application contains features with different frontiers. Intent classification saturates on a\nsmall model; multi-document synthesis does not. Splitting traffic by task and optimizing each separately is\nusually the single largest win, which is why routing comes before most other work.\n\n### Keep measuring\nQuality drifts when the provider updates a model or the input mix changes; prices fall; traffic mix\nshifts; a prompt edit makes the model chattier. Re-run the frontier on a cadence and on every change to\nprompt, model or retrieval, and alert on cost per task and p95 in production, not just on total spend.","example":"A small, runnable version of the frontier step. The measurements are illustrative numbers for a\nsupport-ticket triage task, not benchmarks; the structure is what matters.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Config:\n    name: str\n    quality: float        # pass rate on the task's golden set, 0..1\n    p95_ms: int           # end-to-end p95 on a traffic replay\n    cost_per_task: float  # USD per successful task, retries and judge calls included\n\nconfigs = [\n    Config(\"large, long prompt\",     0.94, 3800, 0.0180),\n    Config(\"large, trimmed prompt\",  0.93, 2900, 0.0110),\n    Config(\"small, trimmed prompt\",  0.86,  900, 0.0012),\n    Config(\"small, fine-tuned\",      0.92,  950, 0.0014),\n    Config(\"small -> large cascade\", 0.93, 1400, 0.0038),\n    Config(\"medium, trimmed prompt\", 0.90, 1700, 0.0045),\n]\n\ndef dominated(c, others):\n    return any(o is not c and o.quality >= c.quality and o.p95_ms <= c.p95_ms\n               and o.cost_per_task <= c.cost_per_task for o in others)\n\nfrontier = [c for c in configs if not dominated(c, configs)]\nfeasible = [c for c in frontier if c.quality >= 0.90 and c.p95_ms <= 2000]\nbest = min(feasible, key=lambda c: c.cost_per_task)\nfor c in frontier:\n    print(f\"{c.name:<24} q={c.quality:.2f}  p95={c.p95_ms:>5} ms  ${c.cost_per_task:.4f}/task\")\nprint(\"choose:\", best.name)\n# large, long prompt       q=0.94  p95= 3800 ms  $0.0180/task\n# small, trimmed prompt    q=0.86  p95=  900 ms  $0.0012/task\n# small, fine-tuned        q=0.92  p95=  950 ms  $0.0014/task\n# small -> large cascade   q=0.93  p95= 1400 ms  $0.0038/task\n# choose: small, fine-tuned\n```\n\nTwo configurations vanish immediately: the trimmed large model is dominated by the cascade (same quality,\nfaster, cheaper), and the medium model is dominated by the fine-tuned small one. Under the constraints\n(quality ≥ 0.90, p95 ≤ 2 s) only the fine-tuned small model and the cascade survive, and the fine-tuned\nmodel wins on cost. Note what the table does not show: the one-off cost of producing the fine-tuned model,\nwhich must be amortized over expected volume before the choice is final, and the fact that the cascade's\np95 is driven by the escalated minority, so its p50 is close to the small model's.","tradeoffs":["A cascade cuts average cost but raises tail latency and adds a confidence gate to maintain; with small-model cost *s*, large-model cost *l* and escalation rate *e*, it beats the large model alone only while `s + e·l < l`, i.e. while `e < 1 − s/l`. At a 10× price gap that is 90%; at 2× it is only 50%.","Trimming context and few-shot examples lowers cost and TTFT on every call, but each removed token is a bet that it was not needed; the trade flips the moment the eval shows quality under the floor.","Thinking or reasoning budgets buy real quality on hard inputs and pure waste on easy ones; the trade is favourable only when gated by task difficulty rather than applied uniformly.","Fine-tuning or distilling a small model shifts the whole frontier but front-loads engineering and must be repeated when the task or base model changes; it pays only when monthly savings exceed the amortized effort, which is a volume threshold.","Response caching gives near-zero cost and latency on hits, but is worthless on long-tail inputs and harmful when answers must reflect fresh data; the trade flips with hit rate and staleness tolerance.","Batch endpoints can cut cost substantially for offline work at the price of minutes-to-hours latency; the trade flips the instant a user is waiting on the result."],"pitfalls":["Optimizing cost with no quality metric in place; the symptom is a falling bill followed weeks later by rising escalation and complaint rates that nobody connects to the change.","Reporting mean latency; the symptom is a green dashboard while users report hangs, because the p95 nobody plotted is three times the mean.","Comparing cost per request instead of per successful task; the symptom is a \"cheaper\" model whose retries, judge calls and human review make the total higher.","Evaluating on a handful of hand-picked prompts; the symptom is a configuration that shines in the demo and degrades on the real traffic distribution.","Leaving output length unconstrained; the symptom is cost and decode latency creeping up after a prompt or model change made the model chattier.","Treating the frontier as static; the symptom is a once-optimal setup that became dominated after a provider price cut or model update that nobody re-tested."],"when":"Run the full frontier exercise when a feature first approaches production scale, whenever a new model or\nprice list appears, and whenever quality, p95 or cost per task moves outside its band in monitoring. Skip\nit for throwaway prototypes and low-volume internal tools, where the cost of measuring exceeds the cost of\nbeing wrong, but still write down the quality floor so the floor exists when volume arrives. The day-to-day\nform is a dashboard with three lines per feature (quality on a sampled eval, p95, cost per successful task)\nand a habit of re-running the eval on every prompt, model or retrieval change. In interviews, the expected\ndepth is to name all three axes unprompted, insist on cost per successful task, separate frontier-moving\nlevers from frontier-shifting ones, and derive the cascade break-even condition from the escalation rate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the eval harness, the traffic replay and the per-config measurement; implements routing, caching and output caps; keeps max_tokens, prompt versions and model ids under version control.","architect":"Sets the quality floor and latency budget per feature, decides which levers are allowed (cascade, distillation, batch paths) and signs off the frontier point the product will run on.","fde":"Rebuilds the frontier inside the customer's constraints, where the eval set is their data, the latency budget is their workflow and the cost ceiling is their contract margin, then defends the choice."},"check":{"q":"A feature runs on a large model at p95 of 4 s, $0.02 per task and 94% on its eval. Product wants\np95 under 2 s and cost under $0.005 per task while keeping quality at 90% or better. Which levers do you\ntry, in what order, and how do you know when you are done?","a":"Start with the levers that change the least: trim the prompt, cap and tighten the output, add\nprompt caching for the shared prefix, and re-measure all three numbers on the full eval and a traffic\nreplay. If that does not clear both constraints, route: a small model first with a confidence or verifier\ngate, escalating to the large model, and compute expected cost as small cost plus escalation rate times\nlarge cost, while checking that the escalated requests do not blow the p95. If escalation stays high, the\nfrontier itself must move: distill the large model's outputs into a fine-tuned small model and amortize\nthat effort against monthly savings at the expected volume. Done means a configuration on the frontier\nsatisfies all three constraints on the eval and the replay, with production monitoring of p95 and cost per\nsuccessful task so a regression is caught in days rather than at the next invoice."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which lever shifts the quality–latency–cost frontier rather than moving along it?","options":["Switching to a larger model tier","Distilling the large model's behaviour into a fine-tuned small model","Raising max_tokens so answers are more complete","Adding more few-shot examples to the prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Model tier, output length and prompt length trade one axis for another and stay on the existing frontier. Distillation makes a cheap, fast model genuinely better at this task, so it improves quality without paying in latency or cost: the frontier itself moves."},{"q":"Why is cost per successful task the right cost measure rather than cost per request?","options":["Because providers bill per task rather than per token","Because it includes retries, fallbacks, judge calls and the cost of failed outputs, which per-request cost hides","Because it is easier to compute from the invoice","Because latency is also measured per task"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A cheap model that fails more often incurs retries, verifier calls and human handling; per-request cost makes it look like a win while the business pays more. Per successful task counts what the outcome actually cost."},{"q":"A small model costs $0.001 per task and a large one $0.010. Below what escalation rate does a small-first cascade beat sending everything to the large model?","options":["10%","50%","90%","It always beats it, at any escalation rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cascade cost is 0.001 + e × 0.010, which is below 0.010 only while e < 0.9. Above that, nearly every request pays for both models and the cascade is strictly worse on cost and on tail latency."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","sources":[],"why":"The question arrives with the first five-figure API invoice: \"would it not be cheaper to run this\nourselves?\" The back-of-envelope answer takes a GPU's hourly price, divides by its benchmark tokens per\nsecond times 3,600, gets a cost per million tokens far below the API's list price, and concludes that\nself-hosting is several times cheaper. That calculation is wrong in three independent ways.\n\nFirst, it assumes the GPU is saturated 24 hours a day. Real interactive traffic is diurnal and bursty; you\nprovision for the peak with headroom to protect latency, so the average utilization of what you bought is a\nfraction, and the effective cost per token is the list cost divided by that fraction. Second, the GPU is the\nvisible line, not the biggest one: someone has to build and run the serving stack, carry the pager, upgrade\nthe model and prove on your evals that an open-weight model matches the hosted one. Third, it compares\ndifferent things: a hosted frontier model against an open-weight model, at different quality.\n\nThe opposite reflex, \"never self-host, it is a distraction\", is equally lazy. At sustained high volume the\nfixed cost amortizes until the marginal token is nearly free, and non-cost drivers such as data residency,\nrate limits during bursts, latency control or a model the vendor does not offer can decide the question on\ntheir own. The break-even analysis exists to replace both reflexes with a curve and a sensitivity band.","how":"### Two cost curves\nWrite monthly cost as a function of monthly volume *V* in tokens.\n\n```\nCost_api(V)  = p_in · V_in + p_out · V_out          ≈ p · V   for a stable input/output mix\nCost_self(V) = F + N(V) · G\n```\n\n`p` is the blended price per token at *your* mix (output tokens usually cost several times input tokens,\nso blend per feature, not per company). `F` is the fixed monthly cost that does not scale with volume:\npeople, platform tooling, eval upkeep. `G` is the monthly cost of one GPU replica. `N(V)` is the number of\nreplicas needed to serve *V*, and it is a step function because you cannot buy a third of a GPU.\n\n### Replica count comes from the peak, not the average\n```\navg_rate  = V / seconds_per_month\npeak_rate = avg_rate · peak_to_average · (1 + headroom)\nN(V)      = ceil(peak_rate / T_slo)\n```\n\n`T_slo` is the sustained tokens per second one replica delivers *at your latency target*, measured on your\nsequence lengths, not the throughput at maximum batch. The distinction matters because continuous batching\ntrades per-request latency for aggregate throughput: the batch size that maximizes tokens per second gives a\np95 nobody would ship. The peak-to-average ratio comes from your traffic logs; headroom covers failover and\nthe fact that a replica at 100% load is already past its latency target.\n\nThe achieved utilization is `u = avg_rate / (N · T_slo)`, roughly `1 / (peak_to_average · (1 + headroom))`\nonce N is large. Interactive traffic with a peak-to-average of 2–3 and 20% headroom lands around a third.\nBatch or offline workloads can be scheduled flat and push *u* toward one, which is why self-hosting pays off\nfar earlier for them. Utilization is the single most important number in the whole analysis.\n\n### Effective cost per token and the sign test\n```\nc_self = G / (T_slo · seconds_per_month · u)\n```\n\nCompare `c_self` to `p` before anything else. If `c_self > p` at the utilization you can realistically\nachieve, there is no break-even at any volume: fixed costs only widen the gap. If `c_self < p`, the\ncontinuous approximation of the crossover is `V* ≈ F / (p − c_self)`, and the step function makes the real\ncrossover slightly later. The sign test is what an API price cut attacks: halve `p` and a workload that was\ncomfortably past break-even can be on the wrong side of it permanently.\n\n### What goes into G and F\n`G` is the accelerator plus its share of the node (CPU, memory, local disk, network), ingress and load\nbalancing, and the difference between on-demand and one- or three-year reserved pricing, which is large.\nOn-prem adds amortized hardware, power, cooling, space and replacement of failed parts. `F` is the fraction\nof platform engineers and SREs, the on-call rotation, serving-stack upgrades, security and compliance work,\nobservability tooling, and the recurring eval and fine-tuning work needed to keep quality parity each time\na new open-weight model or a new hosted model appears. `F` is where most \"it would be cheaper\" spreadsheets\ngo wrong, because it is the line nobody wants to write down.\n\n### Quality parity is a cost, not an assumption\nIf the candidate open-weight model scores below the hosted one on your evals, you either pay to close the\ngap (fine-tuning, which adds a one-off cost and a recurring maintenance line to `F`) or you accept lower\nquality and price that through cost per successful task. Either way, compare the two options at equal\nquality or convert the quality gap into money; comparing at unequal quality is comparing nothing.\n\n### Sensitivity, not a point\nThe honest output is a band. Vary utilization between the batch-flat and interactive-bursty cases, vary\n`p` across list and committed-use pricing and a plausible future price cut, and vary `G` between\non-demand and reserved. Time matters too: a reservation locks `G` for its term, while hosted prices have\ntended to fall and hosted models to improve, so a decision that is marginal today is a bet on the next\ntwelve months of price movement.\n\n### Non-cost factors that can decide it alone\nRegulated data that cannot leave a region or a building; rate limits and quotas that cap bursts during\nyour peak; latency control through co-location; a specific open-weight or custom fine-tuned model the\nvendor does not serve; deprecation and lock-in risk. Any one of these can settle the question regardless\nof cost, but run the arithmetic anyway so the premium being paid for the constraint is known and owned.\n\n### The usual answer is hybrid\nSelf-host the predictable base load, where utilization is high and fixed cost amortizes well, and send\nbursts and rare high-complexity requests to the API, which prices variable demand well. Or split by task:\nhigh-volume simple tasks on a self-hosted small model, frontier-quality tasks on the API. The hybrid wins\nbecause it puts each cost structure on the traffic shape it is built for, at the price of running two\nserving paths and two quality baselines.","example":"A runnable version of the model above, with every number illustrative. The output shows the curves crossing\nand, more importantly, how the crossover moves when one assumption changes.\n\n```python\nimport math\nSEC_MONTH = 30 * 24 * 3600\n\ndef api_cost(tokens_m, price_per_m):\n    return tokens_m * price_per_m\n\ndef self_host_cost(tokens_m, gpu_month, tok_per_s_at_slo, peak_to_avg, headroom, fixed):\n    avg_rate = tokens_m * 1e6 / SEC_MONTH                 # tokens/s averaged over the month\n    peak_rate = avg_rate * peak_to_avg * (1 + headroom)   # what you must provision for\n    replicas = max(1, math.ceil(peak_rate / tok_per_s_at_slo))  # cannot buy 0.3 of a GPU\n    return fixed + replicas * gpu_month, replicas\n\n# Illustrative: blended API price $2.00/M tokens; $3,000 per GPU replica-month (reserved);\n# 2,000 tok/s per replica at the latency target; $16,000/month of people and tooling.\nfor label, price, p2a in ((\"interactive\", 2.00, 2.5), (\"flat batch\", 2.00, 1.2), (\"API cut\", 1.00, 2.5)):\n    print(label)\n    for v in (5_000, 20_000, 50_000, 100_000):            # million tokens per month\n        api = api_cost(v, price)\n        sh, n = self_host_cost(v, 3_000, 2_000, p2a, 0.2, 16_000)\n        print(f\"  {v:>7}M  api=${api:>8,.0f}  self=${sh:>8,.0f} ({n:>2} GPUs)  {'self' if sh < api else 'api'} wins\")\n# interactive:  5,000M api=$10,000 self=$25,000 (3)  20,000M api=$40,000 self=$52,000 (12)\n#               50,000M api=$100,000 self=$103,000 (29)  100,000M api=$200,000 self=$190,000 (58)\n# flat batch:   20,000M api=$40,000 self=$34,000 (6) -> self already wins; 50,000M self=$58,000\n# API cut:      100,000M api=$100,000 self=$190,000 (58) -> api wins at every volume shown\n```\n\nRead the three blocks together. With bursty interactive traffic, self-hosting does not win until somewhere\npast 50 billion tokens a month, an API bill around $100,000. Make the load flat and the same GPUs serve\nroughly twice the tokens, so the crossover drops below 20 billion. Halve the API price and the sign test\nfails: the self-hosted cost per token is now above the API price, and no volume rescues it. The GPU price\nnever changed across the three runs; utilization and the competitor's price did all the work.","tradeoffs":["Self-hosting converts variable spend into fixed commitments; it wins when load is high and flat and loses when load is small or spiky, and the flip is governed by achievable utilization, not by GPU price.","Reserved pricing cuts `G` substantially but locks a term; if traffic falls or a better hosted model appears you keep paying for idle capacity, so it is only worth it with confidence in a year or more of demand.","The hosted API delivers frontier quality with zero platform work, but prices, quotas, regions and deprecations are the vendor's decisions; the trade flips when a quota or residency rule blocks you outright.","Hybrid serving captures the best cost structure for each traffic shape but doubles the surface area: two serving paths, two eval baselines and a routing layer that must keep them consistent.","Matching hosted quality with an open-weight model often needs fine-tuning; that cost is front-loaded and recurs with each base-model generation, which favours the API when model turnover is fast."],"pitfalls":["Using benchmark peak throughput for capacity; the symptom is a plan for 20 GPUs that becomes 60 once p95 latency targets are enforced in load tests.","Omitting people and platform cost; the symptom is a GPU bill below the API bill and a total cost above it, discovered when the headcount request lands.","Comparing against API list price when committed-use discounts exist; the symptom is a break-even computed against a price nobody at your volume actually pays.","Treating the crossover as permanent; the symptom is a self-hosting decision that looked right at one price per million tokens and looks wrong after the next provider price cut.","Ignoring the input/output mix; the symptom is decode-heavy traffic achieving far less than the measured throughput, because decode is bandwidth-bound while the benchmark was prefill-heavy.","Arguing cost when the real driver is residency or quotas; the symptom is weeks of spreadsheet debate while the compliance blocker that actually decides the question goes unaddressed."],"when":"Run the analysis when monthly API spend becomes material, a useful threshold being when it approaches the\nfully loaded cost of one engineer, and again whenever a non-cost driver appears: a residency requirement, a\nquota ceiling during peaks, a vendor deprecation notice. Collect the inputs before opening a spreadsheet:\ntokens per month by feature with the input/output split, the peak-to-average ratio from logs, the latency\ntarget, throughput per replica at that target measured on a candidate open-weight model with your sequence\nlengths, the quality delta on your evals, reserved GPU quotes, and a realistic people plan. The output is a\nbreak-even band under sensitivity and a recommendation that names the premium being paid for whichever\nside is chosen. In interviews, the structural answer is expected: fixed versus variable cost, the\nutilization-driven effective cost per token, the sign test, and the hybrid shape.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Measures throughput at the latency target on real sequence lengths, extracts the peak-to-average ratio from traffic logs, and keeps the calculator honest with production numbers.","architect":"Owns the decision and its band: sets the utilization assumption, the reservation term, the quality-parity requirement and the hybrid split, and records the non-cost drivers that override the arithmetic.","fde":"Rebuilds the break-even with the customer's volumes, their cloud pricing and their compliance constraints, and explains why their \"GPUs are cheaper\" slide omitted the people and utilization lines."},"check":{"q":"Your API bill is $40,000 a month at a blended $2 per million tokens, so 20 billion tokens a month.\nAn engineer says one GPU does 2,000 tokens per second, so four GPUs at $3,000 a month, $12,000 in total,\nwould replace it. What is wrong with that, and what is the right calculation?","a":"Twenty billion tokens a month is roughly 7,700 tokens per second on average, and four GPUs at\n2,000 tokens per second cover that only if they are saturated every second of the month with no headroom.\nWith a peak-to-average ratio of 2.5 and 20% headroom you must provision about 23,000 tokens per second,\nwhich is twelve replicas, $36,000, before adding the platform engineer, on-call and eval upkeep that make\nit about $52,000, above the $40,000 API bill. The 2,000 figure must also be re-measured at the latency\ntarget on your sequence lengths rather than at maximum batch, and the open-weight model must match the\nhosted one on your evals or the gap must be priced. Then run the sensitivity: a flat batch workload drops\nthe replica count to around six and self-hosting wins, while a committed-use API discount to $1.50 per\nmillion makes the API $30,000 and widens its lead. The honest recommendation is to measure peak-to-average\nand throughput at SLO first, and to consider a hybrid that self-hosts the flat base load and sends bursts\nto the API."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which single parameter most changes the self-hosted cost per token?","options":["The GPU's hourly list price","The utilization actually achieved after provisioning for peak with headroom","The model's parameter count","The number of transformer layers in the model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Effective cost per token is the replica cost divided by the tokens it actually serves. Provisioning for a bursty peak leaves most of the capacity idle most of the time, which multiplies the cost per token far more than any plausible difference in GPU price."},{"q":"At realistic utilization the self-hosted effective cost per token comes out above the API's price per token. What does that imply?","options":["Break-even will be reached at a high enough volume","There is no break-even at any volume, because the fixed costs only widen the gap","Break-even is reached at a low volume","More GPUs should be added to lower the cost per token"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the variable part alone is more expensive than the API, adding fixed costs for people and platform makes self-hosting worse at every volume. Only a change in utilization, GPU price or API price can restore a crossover."},{"q":"Why is a hybrid, self-hosting the base load and sending bursts to the API, so often the cheapest shape?","options":["Hosted APIs are always cheaper per token than any self-hosted setup","Fixed capacity amortizes best on flat, predictable load, while bursts are exactly what per-token pricing handles well","It removes the need to evaluate the open-weight model","Self-hosted GPUs cannot technically serve bursty traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each cost structure is matched to the traffic shape it suits: the reserved GPUs run near full utilization on the steady base, and the API absorbs the spiky remainder without idle capacity being paid for. The price is two serving paths to keep consistent."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/finops","sources":["Official docs: FinOps Foundation (FinOps Framework)","Storment & Fuller, 2019 — Cloud FinOps"],"why":"Traditional budgeting assumes spending is decided by purchase orders that finance approves in advance.\nCloud broke that assumption: a line in a config file decides spend, and the invoice arrives a month later.\nLLM APIs break it harder. Spend is per token, so a prompt edit, a retrieval change that doubles context, an\nagent loop that retries, or a traffic spike can move the bill by a multiple with no procurement event and no\ncode reviewer thinking about money. And the provider's invoice is a single line, \"API usage\", that says\nnothing about which product, customer, feature or team caused it. Finance cannot allocate it; engineering\ngets no feedback; the two sides meet only when the number is already alarming.\n\nThe naive responses each fail. Approval gates for every new model or key slow delivery and get bypassed\nwith personal keys, which makes attribution worse. A one-time \"cost project\" saves a slice and then the bill\nclimbs back, because nothing structural changed and nobody is watching. Hard spend caps protect the budget\nby taking the feature down for customers at month end. What actually works is the thing that worked for\nlatency and reliability: timely, attributed data in front of the people making the decisions, clear\nownership, and a loop that runs every week rather than once a year.","how":"### The loop: inform, optimize, operate\nThe FinOps Foundation frames the practice as a cycle. *Inform* means seeing and allocating spend\naccurately and quickly. *Optimize* means acting on it, through both usage and rate levers. *Operate* means\nmaking the first two continuous: budgets, forecasts, reviews, owners and policy. It is a loop *because*\ncosts drift: models are updated, prices change, features ship weekly and traffic mix moves. A snapshot\ngoes stale in a month.\n\n### Inform: meter at the point of call\nProvider invoices lack your context, so attribution has to happen in your own code, at the single point\nevery call passes through: an AI gateway or an SDK wrapper. Each call records the model, input, output and\ncached token counts, latency, tenant, feature, environment, prompt version and a request id that joins to\nyour traces. Cost is computed at record time from a versioned price table and stored alongside the price\nused, *because* prices change and a cost history that silently re-prices itself is useless for trend\nanalysis. Events flow to the warehouse where every other business metric lives.\n\nThe gateway is the structural fix for the root cause of unattributable spend: one shared API key per\ncompany. Per-team or per-feature keys plus mandatory tags make unallocated spend visible as a bucket to be\ndriven toward zero. Start with *showback*, showing each team its own spend, and move to *chargeback*,\nactually debiting internal budgets, only once allocation is trusted; chargeback on shaky numbers produces\narguments about the numbers instead of decisions about the spend.\n\n### Unit economics: telling growth from waste\nTotal spend is the wrong headline. The question is whether cost per unit of value moved: cost per\nsuccessful task, per active user, per document processed, gross margin per tenant. A bill that doubled while\ncost per task stayed flat is growth; a bill that doubled on flat volume is a regression. Only the unit view\ndistinguishes them, and only the unit view lets product price the feature.\n\n### Optimize: usage levers and rate levers are different jobs\n*Usage optimization* means consuming fewer units: prompt trimming, prompt and response caching, routing\ncheap models first, output caps, batch endpoints for offline work, step and token budgets on agents,\nretiring idle GPU replicas. *Rate optimization* means paying less per unit: committed-use and provisioned\nthroughput discounts, reserved GPUs, negotiated enterprise pricing, batch pricing tiers. They are kept\nseparate because they belong to different people (engineering versus procurement), move on different\ntimescales, and mixing them hides which one is actually working.\n\n### Operate: budgets, anomalies, forecasts, owners\nEach feature and team gets a budget with an alert on *forecast* (month-to-date burn rate projected to\nmonth end), not just on the threshold, because by the time a threshold fires the money is spent. Daily\nanomaly detection runs per dimension: a feature whose tokens per task tripled overnight is a bug, not a\ntrend. A named owner reviews each cost centre weekly. Guardrails live in the gateway: per-key spend\nlimits, model allowlists, default output caps. New features carry a cost estimate through design review,\nexpected tokens per task times expected volume, so cost is discussed before the code exists.\n\n### What is specific to AI spend\nTokens are driven by prompt design, so cost is a code-review concern. Agents multiply spend: a ten-step\nloop is ten calls, and context grows with every step. Evaluation is real spend: a large eval suite run\nagainst a judge model on every pull request can rival production traffic. Cached tokens are priced\ndifferently, so cache hit rate is a cost KPI. For self-hosted models the waste metric is GPU idle time,\nwhich belongs on the same dashboard as token spend.","example":"A minimal gateway-side recorder and a daily review that forecasts on burn rate. Prices are placeholders;\nin a real system they are loaded from the provider's current price list and versioned.\n\n```python\nimport time, uuid\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nPRICES = {  # USD per 1M tokens, illustrative; load and version from the provider's price page\n    \"large-model\": {\"in\": 3.00, \"out\": 15.00, \"cached_in\": 0.30},\n    \"small-model\": {\"in\": 0.15, \"out\": 0.60,  \"cached_in\": 0.015},\n}\nEVENTS = []\n\ndef record_call(model, usage, *, tenant, feature, env, prompt_version):\n    p = PRICES[model]\n    cost = (usage[\"in\"] * p[\"in\"] + usage[\"out\"] * p[\"out\"] + usage[\"cached_in\"] * p[\"cached_in\"]) / 1e6\n    event = {\"ts\": time.time(), \"id\": str(uuid.uuid4()), \"model\": model, **usage, \"cost_usd\": cost,\n             \"tenant\": tenant, \"feature\": feature, \"env\": env, \"prompt_version\": prompt_version,\n             \"price_version\": \"2026-10\"}   # store the price used so history stays reproducible\n    EVENTS.append(event)\n    return event\n\ndef daily_review(events, budgets_usd, day_of_month, days_in_month=30):\n    spend = defaultdict(float)\n    for e in events:\n        spend[e[\"feature\"]] += e[\"cost_usd\"]\n    for feature, budget in budgets_usd.items():\n        mtd = spend[feature]\n        forecast = mtd / day_of_month * days_in_month        # burn-rate forecast, not a threshold\n        flag = \"OVER-FORECAST\" if forecast > budget else \"ok\"\n        print(f\"{feature:<10} mtd=${mtd:8.2f}  forecast=${forecast:8.2f}  budget=${budget:6.0f}  {flag}\")\n\n# Illustrative traffic after a retrieval change doubled the context sent by 'search'\nfor _ in range(250_000):\n    record_call(\"small-model\", {\"in\": 2400, \"out\": 150, \"cached_in\": 800},\n                tenant=\"acme\", feature=\"search\", env=\"prod\", prompt_version=\"v12\")\nfor _ in range(50_000):\n    record_call(\"large-model\", {\"in\": 1200, \"out\": 300, \"cached_in\": 0},\n                tenant=\"acme\", feature=\"summarize\", env=\"prod\", prompt_version=\"v3\")\ndaily_review(EVENTS, {\"search\": 300, \"summarize\": 1500}, day_of_month=10)\n# search     mtd=$  115.50  forecast=$  346.50  budget=$   300  OVER-FORECAST\n# summarize  mtd=$  405.00  forecast=$ 1215.00  budget=$  1500  ok\n```\n\nThe point of the output is the first line. On day ten the search feature has spent only $115 of its\n$300 budget, so a threshold alert would stay silent for two more weeks; the burn-rate forecast flags it\ntoday. Because every event carries `prompt_version` and token counts, the next query, tokens per call by\nprompt version, shows that `v12` doubled input tokens, and the fix is a prompt rollback rather than a\nbudget increase.","tradeoffs":["A gateway chokepoint gives complete attribution and enforcement, but it is one more hop of latency and a single point of failure; the trade flips when teams need provider features the gateway cannot pass through.","Chargeback creates real accountability, but needs allocation that teams trust; until allocation is nearly complete, showback produces better decisions with fewer arguments.","Hard per-key spend caps stop runaway bills but can take a customer-facing feature down mid-month; soft forecast alerts plus rate limiting suit production paths, hard caps suit development and evaluation keys.","Commitments such as reserved GPUs or provisioned throughput lower unit price but lock in volume; they pay off only when the baseline is predictable for the whole term.","Fine-grained tags (tenant, feature, prompt version) answer \"which customer is unprofitable\" but demand discipline on every call site; coarse tags are cheap and answer almost nothing."],"pitfalls":["One shared API key for the whole company; the symptom is finance asking who spent the money and nobody being able to answer beyond \"the AI team\".","Alerting only on a monthly threshold; the symptom is an alert that fires on the 28th, after the budget is gone and before anyone can react.","Reporting total spend instead of unit cost; the symptom is a growth month triggering a panic cost cut that damages the product.","Hard-coding prices and never updating them; the symptom is an internal dashboard that disagrees with the invoice by a wide margin and loses everyone's trust.","Ignoring non-production spend; the symptom is production explaining half the bill while evals, load tests and developer experiments own the rest with no owner.","Agents without step and token budgets; the symptom is a single stuck loop becoming the largest line item of the day."],"when":"Start the day there is a bill worth caring about, which for most teams is the first month the API invoice\nexceeds a few hundred dollars or the first GPU reservation. The minimum viable version is one structured\nevent per call with tags, a versioned price table and a daily report by feature; budgets, forecasts,\nanomaly detection and showback follow as spend grows, and chargeback comes last. Measure the practice\nitself: percentage of spend allocated, cost per successful task by feature, cache hit rate, GPU utilization,\nforecast error and time from anomaly to detection. Day to day it shows up as a cost column in the trace\nviewer, a cost estimate in design reviews and a weekly ten-minute review. In interviews, be able to explain\nthe loop, why attribution must happen at the gateway, the usage-versus-rate distinction and why unit cost\nrather than total spend is the headline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments every call with tags and token counts, keeps the price table versioned, adds step and token budgets to agents, and treats a tokens-per-task regression like a latency regression.","architect":"Owns the gateway as the enforcement point, the allocation model, the budget structure and the policy that no feature ships without a cost estimate and an owner.","fde":"Sets up per-tenant metering and showback inside the customer's environment, and turns the first surprising invoice into a cost-per-task conversation about margin rather than a cancellation."},"check":{"q":"The monthly LLM bill jumped from $20,000 to $45,000 and leadership wants a 30% cut by Friday. What\ndo you do first, and why might cutting be the wrong move?","a":"Attribute before acting. Break the $25,000 delta down by feature, tenant, model and prompt version,\nand compute tokens per task and cost per successful task before and after the jump. Three outcomes are\npossible. If volume grew and unit cost is flat, the increase is growth, and the right response is to show\nmargin per task and leave the feature alone. If tokens per task jumped in one feature, it is a regression,\ntypically a retrieval change, a prompt edit or an agent loop retrying, and fixing it recovers most of the\ndelta without touching anything else. If the model or price mix changed, the lever is rate: routing,\ncommitted-use pricing or a tier change. Then install the guardrails that were missing: per-feature budgets\nwith burn-rate forecasts, daily anomaly alerts on tokens per task, per-key limits and a cost estimate in\ndesign review, so the next jump is caught in a day rather than on the invoice."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must token and cost attribution happen in your own gateway rather than from the provider's invoice?","options":["Because provider invoices arrive too late to be useful","Because the invoice carries none of your context: tenant, feature, environment or prompt version","Because providers round costs to the nearest dollar","Because gateways compute costs faster than providers do"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The provider sees API keys and tokens, not your product structure. Only the code that makes the call knows which customer and feature it serves, so the tags have to be attached there and the cost computed alongside them."},{"q":"Which of these is a usage-optimization lever rather than a rate-optimization lever?","options":["Signing a committed-use discount with the provider","Negotiating enterprise pricing for the large model","Enabling prompt caching so repeated prefixes consume fewer billable input tokens","Reserving GPU instances for a one-year term"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rate levers change the price paid per unit and belong to procurement. Caching reduces the number of billable units consumed, which is an engineering change to usage; keeping the two apart shows which lever is working."},{"q":"Total spend doubled this month while cost per successful task stayed flat. What is the FinOps reading?","options":["Waste doubled and spend should be cut immediately","Usage doubled; check margin per task and whether the growth is wanted, rather than cutting","Provider prices doubled without notice","Cost attribution is broken and the numbers cannot be trusted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Flat unit cost with doubled total means twice as many successful tasks were delivered at the same efficiency. That is growth, and the decision belongs to product and pricing, not to a cost cut that would reduce the value delivered."}]}]});
